WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.108
<c.p>[Begin Tape 4551.

2
00:00:01.108 --> 00:00:02.216
Begin Session I.]

3
00:00:02.216 --> 00:00:02.955
<v STEPHANIE DRAGOON>Okay, great.

4
00:00:02.955 --> 00:00:03.694
Okay, awesome.

5
00:00:03.694 --> 00:00:05.541
Today is December seventh, 2015.

6
00:00:05.541 --> 00:00:09.604
We are at the Joshua Rigmaiden Recreation Center in Mossville, Louisiana.

7
00:00:09.604 --> 00:00:16.252
I'm Stephanie Dragoon with the T. Harry Williams Center for Oral History with LSU [Louisiana State University] Libraries.

8
00:00:16.252 --> 00:00:21.793
And I'm here today with Coach LaSalle Williams to talk about his memories of Mossville,

9
00:00:21.793 --> 00:00:29.180
Louisiana as part of a project conducted in conjunction with the Imperial Calcasieu Museum to document the history of Mossville.

10
00:00:29.180 --> 00:00:33.243
This is Coach Williams' second interview, and our first interview together.

11
00:00:33.243 --> 00:00:41.000
Thank you so much for your time and for your willingness to come back and do another interview with us.

12
00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:42.439
<v LASALLE WILLIAMS>You're quite welcome.

13
00:00:42.439 --> 00:00:50.116
<v DRAGOON>To pick up where we left off, where you left off with Chelsea [Arseneault] last interview,

14
00:00:50.116 --> 00:00:58.753
I was wondering if you could tell me more about the wood yard your father owned and operated.

15
00:00:58.753 --> 00:01:01.152
<v WILLIAMS>Well my father was independent.

16
00:01:01.152 --> 00:01:05.470
He had his own business, and basically he farmed.

17
00:01:05.470 --> 00:01:12.187
Also, he had about six acres of property that he farmed behind our house.

18
00:01:12.187 --> 00:01:16.025
But his main business was in wood sales.

19
00:01:16.025 --> 00:01:19.864
And at that time, people had wood stoves.

20
00:01:19.864 --> 00:01:21.303
They didn't have

21
00:01:21.303 --> 00:01:23.702
<c.p>stoves like they have today.

22
00:01:23.702 --> 00:01:26.101
Everybody had a wood stove.

23
00:01:26.101 --> 00:01:28.020
And they had fireplaces.

24
00:01:28.020 --> 00:01:37.616
So most of his wood was sold to, I say, whites who have . . . Because we didn't have too many blacks.

25
00:01:37.616 --> 00:01:40.975
I don't remember Mossville having no fireplaces.

26
00:01:40.975 --> 00:01:44.813
So he sold wood, primarily oak and hickory.

27
00:01:44.813 --> 00:01:48.172
And he had his own wood yard.

28
00:01:48.172 --> 00:01:55.369
And that's where I learned how to cut wood and learned what a half rank,

29
00:01:55.369 --> 00:02:01.606
and a rank, and a cord and all that, I learned from him.

30
00:02:01.606 --> 00:02:08.323
And this is where he made a living for himself and for the family.

31
00:02:08.323 --> 00:02:11.202
And, of course, he adopted me.

32
00:02:11.202 --> 00:02:16.000
I was adopted into that family, the Williams family.

33
00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:19.970
I was born in Houston, Texas in 1931.

34
00:02:19.970 --> 00:02:27.415
Well, they say they brought me here, Mossville, when I was about six years old.

35
00:02:27.415 --> 00:02:36.844
My biological mother was raised by the people who adopt me, and that's how I got in the family.

36
00:02:36.844 --> 00:02:39.822
And it's where I grew up.

37
00:02:39.822 --> 00:02:43.793
Like I said, my father had a farm.

38
00:02:43.793 --> 00:02:46.274
We raised everything, just about.

39
00:02:46.274 --> 00:02:49.748
He had ducks, hogs, chickens, and cows.

40
00:02:49.748 --> 00:02:51.237
You name it.

41
00:02:51.237 --> 00:03:00.667
And plus he planted beans, corn, and so we made a great living with food and supplies from that.

42
00:03:00.667 --> 00:03:04.141
I went to school here in Mossville.

43
00:03:04.141 --> 00:03:05.630
Had one school.

44
00:03:05.630 --> 00:03:13.074
Matter of fact, that school is located on the site right here where we at.

45
00:03:13.074 --> 00:03:21.015
And it was a large building with two rooms separated in the middle with a partition.

46
00:03:21.015 --> 00:03:27.963
Well you had classes going on one side and classes on the other side.

47
00:03:27.963 --> 00:03:31.933
But they only went to the sixth grade.

48
00:03:31.933 --> 00:03:39.378
And the first principal was . . . his name was Williams, but not related to my family.

49
00:03:39.378 --> 00:03:44.837
And like I say, we only went to the sixth grade.

50
00:03:44.837 --> 00:03:53.274
When it considered then, the sixth grade in Mossville concerned you was graduated equivalent to twelfth grade.

51
00:03:53.274 --> 00:04:00.222
But now right down the street in Westlake [Louisiana], they had a high school.

52
00:04:00.222 --> 00:04:07.170
But it was segregated, and at that time they went to the ninth grade.

53
00:04:07.170 --> 00:04:10.644
But eventually it went to twelfth grade.

54
00:04:10.644 --> 00:04:17.096
So what we had to do was we got . . . most kids in Mossville,

55
00:04:17.096 --> 00:04:22.556
when they got through sixth grade, they got a job somewhere.

56
00:04:22.556 --> 00:04:26.030
Some went in the military or whatever.

57
00:04:26.030 --> 00:04:31.985
But my parent wanted to see me have a high school education.

58
00:04:31.985 --> 00:04:36.948
So what they did, they drove me to Lake Charles

59
00:04:36.948 --> 00:04:39.926
<c.p>[Louisiana] with my brother and sister.

60
00:04:39.926 --> 00:04:43.896
And we didn't have a parish high school.

61
00:04:43.896 --> 00:04:45.881
It was a city.

62
00:04:45.881 --> 00:04:50.348
And the school was furnished by city tax payers.

63
00:04:50.348 --> 00:04:57.296
But we weren’t allowed to go because we was outside of the city limits.

64
00:04:57.296 --> 00:04:59.778
We was in the parish.

65
00:04:59.778 --> 00:05:11.193
So I had relatives in Lake Charles, so we had to use the relatives' address in order to stay and go to school.

66
00:05:11.193 --> 00:05:15.163
Otherwise they was going to put us out.

67
00:05:15.163 --> 00:05:21.615
And I started at Second Ward in the seventh and the eighth grade.

68
00:05:21.615 --> 00:05:33.030
Then they . . . I think it was about, yeah, the third year, they opened W. O. Boston High School for blacks in Lake Charles.

69
00:05:33.030 --> 00:05:37.000
And still that was in the city.

70
00:05:37.000 --> 00:05:41.664
So I went from Second Ward to W. O. Boston.

71
00:05:41.664 --> 00:05:49.126
But the funny thing about it, at the same time someone, I don't know what company,

72
00:05:49.126 --> 00:05:55.188
probably company put on a bus to go from Lake Charles to Mossville.

73
00:05:55.188 --> 00:06:05.448
It went all the way up here past Coach Williams Drive and picked up people going shopping in Lake Charles, people working.

74
00:06:05.448 --> 00:06:07.780
Very few people had automobiles.

75
00:06:07.780 --> 00:06:10.112
My parents always had one.

76
00:06:10.112 --> 00:06:17.574
So we started catching the bus, see, because the bus was running five times a day.

77
00:06:17.574 --> 00:06:27.834
And it run early that morning at six o' clock, and then the last run was at ten o' clock that night.

78
00:06:27.834 --> 00:06:39.960
So the principal started watching that bus because the bus that we was riding was located over there by the main bus station, Greyhound bus station.

79
00:06:39.960 --> 00:06:44.623
But it was across the street where we catching it.

80
00:06:44.623 --> 00:06:53.018
So he started watching it, and some of them he called and he put them out of school.

81
00:06:53.018 --> 00:06:55.816
But they started an athletic program.

82
00:06:55.816 --> 00:06:58.614
Football, track at that new school.

83
00:06:58.614 --> 00:07:05.143
And I had no idea how fast I could run or my athletic ability.

84
00:07:05.143 --> 00:07:11.206
So when they found out, and I had the top . . . Matter of fact,

85
00:07:11.206 --> 00:07:16.803
I held the top record in the hundred yard dash for years.

86
00:07:16.803 --> 00:07:21.000
And I mean, that was all over Lake Charles.

87
00:07:21.000 --> 00:07:22.865
Including white and black.

88
00:07:22.865 --> 00:07:29.861
But when they found out what kind of athlete I was, they didn't bother me.

89
00:07:29.861 --> 00:07:32.659
I'd catch the bus every day.

90
00:07:32.659 --> 00:07:35.457
And that's where I graduated from.

91
00:07:35.457 --> 00:07:36.390
Playing football.

92
00:07:36.390 --> 00:07:39.188
Lettered in both football and track.

93
00:07:39.188 --> 00:07:43.852
And I had two scholarships: one to go to Grambling

94
00:07:43.852 --> 00:07:48.982
<c.p>[State University; Grambling, LA], and one to go to Wiley College.

95
00:07:48.982 --> 00:07:55.045
I don't know if y'all know where Wiley is, that's in [Marshall,] Texas.

96
00:07:55.045 --> 00:07:58.309
And my track coach graduated from Wiley.

97
00:07:58.309 --> 00:08:00.641
See, he was an alumni.

98
00:08:00.641 --> 00:08:07.637
So that's how he encouraged me to go and looked at the campus and walk,

99
00:08:07.637 --> 00:08:13.700
stayed over there a few days, so that's where I decided to go.

100
00:08:13.700 --> 00:08:20.695
Well, at the same time, that's when that Korean Conflict . . . and they went to drafting.

101
00:08:20.695 --> 00:08:29.090
And I had signed up, well, everybody had to sign up then once you got eighteen years old.

102
00:08:29.090 --> 00:08:33.287
So the lady really didn't like me too well.

103
00:08:33.287 --> 00:08:40.749
Her name was, the draft lady, and anybody that you talked to that know her, [. . .?] [nightmares?].

104
00:08:40.749 --> 00:08:42.148
She was tough.

105
00:08:42.148 --> 00:08:45.413
Bigger white lady, but she was tough.

106
00:08:45.413 --> 00:08:54.740
And I tried to get an excuse from the military to go to college, and she say, “No. You going."

107
00:08:54.740 --> 00:08:58.471
And that's where I had to go to.

108
00:08:58.471 --> 00:09:00.336
And I enjoyed it.

109
00:09:00.336 --> 00:09:01.735
I went military.

110
00:09:01.735 --> 00:09:05.000
When I got in the military . . .

111
00:09:05.000 --> 00:09:05.767
<v DRAGOON>Oh, sorry.

112
00:09:05.767 --> 00:09:07.301
What year was that?

113
00:09:07.301 --> 00:09:10.753
<v WILLIAMS>It was in nineteen . . . See, I graduated in 1952.

114
00:09:10.753 --> 00:09:13.055
That's when I had to go.

115
00:09:13.055 --> 00:09:18.808
Me and another one of my wife's cousins were both drafted at the same time.

116
00:09:18.808 --> 00:09:23.411
And I went in the military, spent my time, went to school.

117
00:09:23.411 --> 00:09:25.329
I went to two schools.

118
00:09:25.329 --> 00:09:29.548
I went to demolition school, and I went to heavy equipment.

119
00:09:29.548 --> 00:09:31.849
When I was in the military.

120
00:09:31.849 --> 00:09:38.370
Well, at the end of my two years, they had talked me into going to O.C.S. school.

121
00:09:38.370 --> 00:09:42.973
And that's where I was going to go to be a lieutenant.

122
00:09:42.973 --> 00:09:46.425
And still, I had to re-up in the military.

123
00:09:46.425 --> 00:09:54.096
So my mama that adopted me, she said, “No, I want you to come home, and you go to college."

124
00:09:54.096 --> 00:10:01.384
And I didn't want to do that, because at the time, I married while I was in the military.

125
00:10:01.384 --> 00:10:07.904
Well, I had the rank of a staff sergeant, which was a good salary in the military.

126
00:10:07.904 --> 00:10:09.822
Plus, I had two allotments.

127
00:10:09.822 --> 00:10:15.959
I had allotment coming home to my wife, and I had allotment coming home to my

128
00:10:15.959 --> 00:10:16.342
<c.p>mama.

129
00:10:16.342 --> 00:10:22.096
So I had found out I was going to use the military as a career.

130
00:10:22.096 --> 00:10:23.630
That was my object.

131
00:10:23.630 --> 00:10:29.000
She said, “No.” She told me that and I come got my scholarship.

132
00:10:29.000 --> 00:10:32.167
Went on to Grambling, started running track.

133
00:10:32.167 --> 00:10:37.145
And the same time, same year, that's when the colleges integrated.

134
00:10:37.145 --> 00:10:43.932
Well, it just so happened the white track coach at McNeese [State University; Lake Charles,

135
00:10:43.932 --> 00:10:53.434
LA] knew about me because all our track meets, we didn’t have a track of our own at the black school.

136
00:10:53.434 --> 00:10:57.507
We had to run at Lake Charles High School.

137
00:10:57.507 --> 00:11:07.009
So he saw what kind of athletic ability I had, so when the schools integrated, he talked me into coming back.

138
00:11:07.009 --> 00:11:13.796
Him and one of the local . . . Lon Tyndall was a representative . . . white representative, state representative.

139
00:11:13.796 --> 00:11:22.394
He knew my family, so he talked me, too, and gave me a legislation scholarship to come to McNeese.

140
00:11:22.394 --> 00:11:30.086
And I came back to McNeese, and that's where I got into sports, and I finished McNeese.

141
00:11:30.086 --> 00:11:36.873
And when I graduated from McNeese, I got a head coaching job the same year.

142
00:11:36.873 --> 00:11:45.471
I found out that that's unusual because usually when you graduated from college, to get in the coaching business,

143
00:11:45.471 --> 00:11:51.805
you got to be an assistant coach before you can become a head coach.

144
00:11:51.805 --> 00:12:02.213
So I got the head coaching job at Mossville High School, and that's where I coached for ten years until the schools integrated.

145
00:12:02.213 --> 00:12:09.000
So I was transferred to McNeese . . . I mean to Westlake as an assistant coach.

146
00:12:09.000 --> 00:12:12.121
But now getting back to high school.

147
00:12:12.121 --> 00:12:15.241
I may be kind of rambling around.

148
00:12:15.241 --> 00:12:20.146
When they put that bus on, show you something about segregation.

149
00:12:20.146 --> 00:12:25.495
We had to pass right by a white school in the bus.

150
00:12:25.495 --> 00:12:31.737
Every day, we go to Lake Charles across the bridge to go to school.

151
00:12:31.737 --> 00:12:34.412
That's just how bad it was.

152
00:12:34.412 --> 00:12:42.882
But when I went to Westlake, I was promised to get the head coach job when it became available.

153
00:12:42.882 --> 00:12:45.557
Well, through politics, that didn't happen.

154
00:12:45.557 --> 00:12:55.811
They used the excuse that well, you got to have a master’s degree to become a head coach . . . as in the white school.

155
00:12:55.811 --> 00:12:57.594
Well, I knew some

156
00:12:57.594 --> 00:13:03.390
<c.p>schools didn’t have, had white coaches but they didn't have no master’s degree.

157
00:13:03.390 --> 00:13:07.848
And for example, right over there in Moss Bluff [Louisiana].

158
00:13:07.848 --> 00:13:11.415
But still, that's what they used against me.

159
00:13:11.415 --> 00:13:16.319
So what I did, I went and got a master’s degree.

160
00:13:16.319 --> 00:13:18.102
I went and enrolled.

161
00:13:18.102 --> 00:13:23.898
And when I got the master’s, I continued and got a plus thirty.

162
00:13:23.898 --> 00:13:31.923
So when that happened, they saw then that I was in a position to become a head coach.

163
00:13:31.923 --> 00:13:35.489
Well they made a principal out of me.

164
00:13:35.489 --> 00:13:45.297
Well, if I'd have turned it down, which mean get in politics and school system, you probably wouldn't get that opportunity again.

165
00:13:45.297 --> 00:13:54.659
So they gave me the opportunity to become a principal and gave me more money, so I was principal at Mossville.

166
00:13:54.659 --> 00:14:00.009
And that was a middle school because they closed the high school.

167
00:14:00.009 --> 00:14:07.142
And then in later years, they closed the elementary school because of the lack of enrollment.

168
00:14:07.142 --> 00:14:11.601
That's when they started that bussing with freedom of choice.

169
00:14:11.601 --> 00:14:18.734
They had buses run that take kids to any school their parents wanted them to go.

170
00:14:18.734 --> 00:14:28.096
So that kind of decreased the enrollment kept decreasing, so I became principal then at the school in Lake Charles Boston.

171
00:14:28.096 --> 00:14:33.000
That's where I stayed for seven years, almost eight years.

172
00:14:33.000 --> 00:14:34.240
<v DRAGOON>Where was that?

173
00:14:34.240 --> 00:14:35.481
<v WILLIAMS>In Lake Charles.

174
00:14:35.481 --> 00:14:36.721
Lake Charles Boston.

175
00:14:36.721 --> 00:14:45.404
It still got Lake Charles Boston, but they call it Lake Charles Academy now because it's no longer a high school.

176
00:14:45.404 --> 00:14:48.712
And I was principal at that P.M. school.

177
00:14:48.712 --> 00:14:51.192
They had two schools in one.

178
00:14:51.192 --> 00:14:53.260
Day and a night school.

179
00:14:53.260 --> 00:15:00.288
But they did, they had the same subjects, had the same graduation, the same high school diplomas.

180
00:15:00.288 --> 00:15:03.596
So I stayed in education for forty-two years.

181
00:15:03.596 --> 00:15:08.144
I retired as a coach at twenty years from Westlake High.

182
00:15:08.144 --> 00:15:16.827
At that time, you could retire twenty years, then I retired twenty-two years as a principal, which gave me forty-two years.

183
00:15:16.827 --> 00:15:18.481
So that's my education.

184
00:15:18.481 --> 00:15:18.894
Generally.

185
00:15:18.894 --> 00:15:20.548
But after that, I

186
00:15:20.548 --> 00:15:20.962
<c.p>retired.

187
00:15:20.962 --> 00:15:29.231
I said well I'm going to get me a boat and I'll fish, and I'll play golf . . . because I play.

188
00:15:29.231 --> 00:15:37.913
I taught golf when I was in . . . But my gardening didn't work out too good because I wasn't a good gardener,

189
00:15:37.913 --> 00:15:39.981
so I start playing golf.

190
00:15:39.981 --> 00:15:43.288
Then my golf game was up and down.

191
00:15:43.288 --> 00:15:47.010
And we was playing like three times a week.

192
00:15:47.010 --> 00:15:52.798
So finally I got interested in becoming . . . Well, the sheriff talked me into it.

193
00:15:52.798 --> 00:15:58.173
Wayne, which is a good friend, he was the sheriff at the time.

194
00:15:58.173 --> 00:16:02.721
He say, "Coach, why don't you come and work with us?

195
00:16:02.721 --> 00:16:05.202
You know all the young people.

196
00:16:05.202 --> 00:16:09.750
These young men from generations so you can help us out.”

197
00:16:09.750 --> 00:16:11.404
Say, “Come part time."

198
00:16:11.404 --> 00:16:18.019
So I decided to go fill out an application and become part-time at the sheriff department.

199
00:16:18.019 --> 00:16:24.635
Well the same year, he came up for re-election, and he got beat by this woman.

200
00:16:24.635 --> 00:16:27.529
And nobody thought she could beat him.

201
00:16:27.529 --> 00:16:29.183
Now look at this.

202
00:16:29.183 --> 00:16:34.144
She was the daughter of the superintendent of the Calcasieu parish schools,

203
00:16:34.144 --> 00:16:42.827
which I had worked under and work . . . So he sent her to me to help her over in the Mossville area.

204
00:16:42.827 --> 00:16:51.923
Because at the time I was president of the NAACP [National Association for the Advancement of Colored People] on West Cal branch,

205
00:16:51.923 --> 00:16:55.644
which was Westlake, Mossville, Sulphur [Louisiana], and Vinton [Louisiana].

206
00:16:55.644 --> 00:16:58.538
So I knew a lot of people.

207
00:16:58.538 --> 00:17:07.635
But I told her when she came to me, I said, "Well, look I'm going to be fair and honest with you.

208
00:17:07.635 --> 00:17:09.288
I'm committed to Wayne.”

209
00:17:09.288 --> 00:17:13.837
I said, “I'm not going to do anything to hurt you.”

210
00:17:13.837 --> 00:17:17.144
I said, "But I'm already committed to him."

211
00:17:17.144 --> 00:17:21.279
But nobody had no idea she was going to win.

212
00:17:21.279 --> 00:17:25.000
So she won, and she pulled my commission.

213
00:17:25.000 --> 00:17:30.968
So anyway, all the police marshal and all that, because they got a marshal commission for Sulphur.

214
00:17:30.968 --> 00:17:37.989
And I helped them out a lot over there with kids and locating them when they was hiding and all.

215
00:17:37.989 --> 00:17:41.851
So they said, "Coach, we going to get your commission back."

216
00:17:41.851 --> 00:17:46.064
I said, “No, I don’t want nothing with her name on it.”

217
00:17:46.064 --> 00:17:47.117
And I didn’t.

218
00:17:47.117 --> 00:17:48.872
So she didn't get reelected.

219
00:17:48.872 --> 00:17:49.223
So

220
00:17:49.223 --> 00:17:54.138
<c.p>Tony, the sheriff present, the sheriff today, he got me up there for part-time.

221
00:17:54.138 --> 00:17:56.596
And I had to take a test.

222
00:17:56.596 --> 00:17:58.351
Well, I didn’t want to.

223
00:17:58.351 --> 00:17:59.404
A written test.

224
00:17:59.404 --> 00:18:02.915
I sais, "Man, I been taking tests all my days.

225
00:18:02.915 --> 00:18:03.968
I don't want."

226
00:18:03.968 --> 00:18:08.883
He said, "Well, you got to take this. [unclear] policies required by the state."

227
00:18:08.883 --> 00:18:12.043
So I went took the test, passed the test.

228
00:18:12.043 --> 00:18:16.255
They called me say, “Well, you got to go have a physical.”

229
00:18:16.255 --> 00:18:20.819
Well it just so happened that the doctor that was doing the physicals,

230
00:18:20.819 --> 00:18:26.787
which is still a doctor for the sheriff department, he was the first white doctor in Westlake.

231
00:18:26.787 --> 00:18:28.894
And I had coached his son.

232
00:18:28.894 --> 00:18:37.670
So when he find out, when I went up there for the physical, we must have talked thirty minutes before we even started the exam.

233
00:18:37.670 --> 00:18:39.426
Anyway, I passed the physical.

234
00:18:39.426 --> 00:18:43.638
And they say, "Well, you got to have a lie detector test."

235
00:18:43.638 --> 00:18:46.447
Said, “I never had a lie detector test.

236
00:18:46.447 --> 00:18:48.202
I been in the military.

237
00:18:48.202 --> 00:18:53.468
I never been arrested, no." So I went took the lie detector test, passed that,

238
00:18:53.468 --> 00:19:00.138
and the next thing they call me in there for my uniform and take a picture for my commission.

239
00:19:00.138 --> 00:19:04.000
And that was supposed to have been for part time.

240
00:19:04.000 --> 00:19:10.887
So the policy is when you start to work at the sheriff department, is you work in the jail first.

241
00:19:10.887 --> 00:19:18.118
And they put you in, you work in different areas to familiarize yourself with the inmates and what you’re dealing with.

242
00:19:18.118 --> 00:19:21.906
So when I was working at night, the twelve hour shifts.

243
00:19:21.906 --> 00:19:26.382
So when I went up there at night, the first night to work,

244
00:19:26.382 --> 00:19:31.203
the supervisor, he put me in the area with him that's in the tower.

245
00:19:31.203 --> 00:19:35.679
And then about a week in there, they move me to another area.

246
00:19:35.679 --> 00:19:40.156
Well, a lot of my former students, white and black, was in jail.

247
00:19:40.156 --> 00:19:43.943
And when they saw me up there, they hid from me.

248
00:19:43.943 --> 00:19:52.208
Well, they couldn’t hide forever because they found out I was up there permanent, so a lot of them come out and they say,

249
00:19:52.208 --> 00:19:58.406
"Coach, we sure hate you to see us up here like this because you tried to help us."

250
00:19:58.406 --> 00:20:03.915
So by the fifth, I think it was the fourth week, they had a racial fight.

251
00:20:03.915 --> 00:20:10.458
So whenever they have a fight, they called all the deputies from the different available deputies to that area.

252
00:20:10.458 --> 00:20:11.146
So they

253
00:20:11.146 --> 00:20:16.311
<c.p>had this little young black boy wouldn't give his name, he wouldn't tell them nothing.

254
00:20:16.311 --> 00:20:21.821
So I pulled him off aside and I talked to him and got him to cooperate.

255
00:20:21.821 --> 00:20:26.297
So I decide and come back to my wife, "This ain't for me."

256
00:20:26.297 --> 00:20:30.774
I say, "Because I been fighting this all my life with them kids.”

257
00:20:30.774 --> 00:20:35.939
I say, “I ain't going up there,” say, “I'm going to get out of this."

258
00:20:35.939 --> 00:20:40.071
So I went back to the . . . Doctor White, got me an excuse.

259
00:20:40.071 --> 00:20:44.203
I went to my doctor, which is my doctor today, Doctor Caine.

260
00:20:44.203 --> 00:20:45.236
He wrote me.

261
00:20:45.236 --> 00:20:51.090
I took it back up there, and they say, "No, we ain't going to let you go."

262
00:20:51.090 --> 00:20:54.533
Say, "We going to put you in a different area."

263
00:20:54.533 --> 00:20:59.354
So they put me back in the area where it wasn't nothing but trustees.

264
00:20:59.354 --> 00:21:04.175
These are trustees, ones that work on the farm, ones that got special detail.

265
00:21:04.175 --> 00:21:06.241
Ones that work at the shop.

266
00:21:06.241 --> 00:21:08.651
Those that go and leave and work.

267
00:21:08.651 --> 00:21:11.061
So I was dealing with nothing then.

268
00:21:11.061 --> 00:21:13.127
It was away from the youngsters.

269
00:21:13.127 --> 00:21:16.226
These are guys that are trying to get out.

270
00:21:16.226 --> 00:21:18.981
And they was getting paid, too, to work.

271
00:21:18.981 --> 00:21:22.080
It's like the ones that work on the farm.

272
00:21:22.080 --> 00:21:25.524
What they do is they take and reduce their time.

273
00:21:25.524 --> 00:21:30.000
They don't get no money for it, but they reduce their time.

274
00:21:30.000 --> 00:21:31.800
<v DRAGOON>Where . . . what prison is . . . are you?

275
00:21:31.800 --> 00:21:32.700
<v WILLIAMS>In Lake Charles.

276
00:21:32.700 --> 00:21:35.400
The correctional center in Lake Charles on Broad Street.

277
00:21:35.400 --> 00:21:36.000
<v DRAGOON>Okay.

278
00:21:36.000 --> 00:21:38.625
<v WILLIAMS>That's the only prison we have here.

279
00:21:38.625 --> 00:21:46.500
We had one in Dequincy, but they closed that one, and they made something like a learning center out of it.

280
00:21:46.500 --> 00:21:48.375
<v DRAGOON>When were you working there?

281
00:21:48.375 --> 00:21:51.000
<v WILLIAMS>I started work there . . . Let me see.

282
00:21:51.000 --> 00:21:52.875
This is my twelfth year.

283
00:21:52.875 --> 00:21:56.250
But I worked in that area for five years.

284
00:21:56.250 --> 00:21:59.250
And then they moved me as a guard.

285
00:21:59.250 --> 00:22:01.125
At the main guard gate.

286
00:22:01.125 --> 00:22:04.875
Me and another deputy, so that's where I been all.

287
00:22:04.875 --> 00:22:09.750
So really I don't have no more dealings with anybody in the prisoners.

288
00:22:09.750 --> 00:22:16.125
So we at the main guard gate where we check people in and out and all that.

289
00:22:16.125 --> 00:22:17.625
And I been there.

290
00:22:17.625 --> 00:22:22.875
So if I go three more years, I'll be able to retire from there.

291
00:22:22.875 --> 00:22:29.250
I went there specially looking for benefits and Social Security because I never did have enough quarters.

292
00:22:29.250 --> 00:22:36.000
Because in the school system, they can do it now, but then you couldn't pay in Social Security.

293
00:22:36.000 --> 00:22:36.750
Only retirement.

294
00:22:36.750 --> 00:22:46.125
So I say, "Well, I go to the sheriff department and work there, and I can make up the other quarters that I'm short of.”

295
00:22:46.125 --> 00:22:52.500
When I got there and found out you couldn't pay in Social Security, that's another retirement program.

296
00:22:52.500 --> 00:22:55.875
So if I’m blessed to do three more years,

297
00:22:55.875 --> 00:23:02.625
I can retire from there and have full medical benefits from both the school and the sheriff department.

298
00:23:02.625 --> 00:23:06.375
Well it's a blessing because I never been seriously ill.

299
00:23:06.375 --> 00:23:13.500
But my wife had two cancer surgeries, she had open heart surgery, and she's going up for another one.

300
00:23:13.500 --> 00:23:15.000
Sometime in December.

301
00:23:15.000 --> 00:23:20.917
<v DRAGOON>You mentioned that you married your wife when you were in the military.

302
00:23:20.917 --> 00:23:22.737
<v WILLIAMS>Yeah, we was married.

303
00:23:22.737 --> 00:23:25.013
Let me tell you what.

304
00:23:25.013 --> 00:23:26.378
Our first child.

305
00:23:26.378 --> 00:23:30.474
I was in the military when she was born.

306
00:23:30.474 --> 00:23:33.660
She's a registered nurse over in [unclear].

307
00:23:33.660 --> 00:23:35.026
Well, we decided.

308
00:23:35.026 --> 00:23:39.122
Said, “Well, we not going to have but two.”

309
00:23:39.122 --> 00:23:44.128
She was going to shoot for a boy and a girl.

310
00:23:44.128 --> 00:23:46.404
Alright, we had the boy.

311
00:23:46.404 --> 00:23:49.135
See, we thought this was it.

312
00:23:49.135 --> 00:23:53.231
Well see, she started McNeese at the same time.

313
00:23:53.231 --> 00:23:53.686
And

314
00:23:53.686 --> 00:23:56.417
<c.p>my parents would keep the kids.

315
00:23:56.417 --> 00:24:00.968
So I was staying with my parents at the time.

316
00:24:00.968 --> 00:24:06.429
So fool around there and she got pregnant for the third one.

317
00:24:06.429 --> 00:24:08.705
And say, this is it.

318
00:24:08.705 --> 00:24:13.712
Just went back to school and she started again at McNeese.

319
00:24:13.712 --> 00:24:16.442
Then up popped the fourth one.

320
00:24:16.442 --> 00:24:19.173
We ain't saying nothing no more.

321
00:24:19.173 --> 00:24:20.083
That's it.

322
00:24:20.083 --> 00:24:24.179
And she went on and she graduated from McNeese.

323
00:24:24.179 --> 00:24:26.000
Got her master's.

324
00:24:26.000 --> 00:24:28.178
<v DRAGOON>What did she study?

325
00:24:28.178 --> 00:24:37.978
<v WILLIAMS>P.E. and social studies, and she worked at [Reinhardt?] Middle School for thirty-four years until she got ill.

326
00:24:37.978 --> 00:24:40.700
But now she's doing fine.

327
00:24:40.700 --> 00:24:45.056
She's a cancer survivor, and she's still subbing.

328
00:24:45.056 --> 00:24:51.044
She's subbing only one school, and that's Western Heights in Westlake.

329
00:24:51.044 --> 00:24:55.400
Matter of fact, she's subbing all this week.

330
00:24:55.400 --> 00:25:00.300
So that kind of took care of my education.

331
00:25:00.300 --> 00:25:03.022
And what we went through.

332
00:25:03.022 --> 00:25:05.744
And we raised four kids.

333
00:25:05.744 --> 00:25:06.833
Four children.

334
00:25:06.833 --> 00:25:10.644
And just one of them is married.

335
00:25:10.644 --> 00:25:12.278
My oldest son.

336
00:25:12.278 --> 00:25:17.178
He got two . . . four grandkids, and two great grandkids.

337
00:25:17.178 --> 00:25:22.622
But now this is the one going to shock you.

338
00:25:22.622 --> 00:25:27.522
We had, what is this, godfather and god mama.

339
00:25:27.522 --> 00:25:32.967
A girl across the street from us on our street.

340
00:25:32.967 --> 00:25:42.222
So her and her husband, they was supposed to been, I guess you call them special ed.

341
00:25:42.222 --> 00:25:47.122
And they had a night club on my street.

342
00:25:47.122 --> 00:25:54.744
Well, they had two but one of them closed after I built the house,

343
00:25:54.744 --> 00:26:04.000
so they had the one that was operated by my auntie from my uncle's first marriage.

344
00:26:04.000 --> 00:26:05.507
<v DRAGOON>What were they called?

345
00:26:05.507 --> 00:26:10.404
<v WILLIAMS>It was called, they called it . . . First it was called the Swamp House.

346
00:26:10.404 --> 00:26:13.041
And they changed the name later on.

347
00:26:13.041 --> 00:26:15.678
Well anyway, this girl was our godchild.

348
00:26:15.678 --> 00:26:17.185
My wife saw her

349
00:26:17.185 --> 00:26:19.822
<c.p>coming out of that club one day.

350
00:26:19.822 --> 00:26:22.459
Because we got a split level house.

351
00:26:22.459 --> 00:26:28.110
Saw her coming out of that club one day with that baby in her arm.

352
00:26:28.110 --> 00:26:35.267
So she called and she told her say, "Look, you don't supposed to bring babies to places like that."

353
00:26:35.267 --> 00:26:39.788
And she found out that the baby had a bad diaper rash.

354
00:26:39.788 --> 00:26:43.555
So my wife say, "Look, let me take this baby."

355
00:26:43.555 --> 00:26:46.945
And baby had never had no shots or nothing.

356
00:26:46.945 --> 00:26:50.336
She took her to get all the baby's shots.

357
00:26:50.336 --> 00:26:56.740
Brought her back, found out that the diaper rash was caused from diaper staying on too long.

358
00:26:56.740 --> 00:27:06.534
So what she did, doctor told her get some regular diapers that you wash and dry out, and give her some little powder medicine or something.

359
00:27:06.534 --> 00:27:10.301
We got the baby all cleared up, staying with us.

360
00:27:10.301 --> 00:27:13.315
Then we brought her back to the mama.

361
00:27:13.315 --> 00:27:18.212
Baby just cried, cried, cried, she didn't want to stay with the mama.

362
00:27:18.212 --> 00:27:20.473
Now this is a baby now.

363
00:27:20.473 --> 00:27:23.110
So the grandma came and got her.

364
00:27:23.110 --> 00:27:26.500
Was staying on Cookie Lane, that's in Lake Charles.

365
00:27:26.500 --> 00:27:27.630
Off Opelousas street.

366
00:27:27.630 --> 00:27:31.021
I don't know if you're familiar with that area.

367
00:27:31.021 --> 00:27:32.527
That's north Lake Charles.

368
00:27:32.527 --> 00:27:39.308
So she called us and told us, "Look, we can't get this baby to settle down for nothing.

369
00:27:39.308 --> 00:27:41.945
She won't eat, won't drink her milk."

370
00:27:41.945 --> 00:27:46.089
Said, "If y'all come, maybe y'all could help settle her down."

371
00:27:46.089 --> 00:27:54.000
We went over there and walked in that house, and time we walked in, that baby just reached for us.

372
00:27:54.000 --> 00:27:57.826
So we brought her, and we adopted her.

373
00:27:57.826 --> 00:28:00.696
And sent her to high school.

374
00:28:00.696 --> 00:28:05.957
She graduated from high school with me over there at L.C.B.

375
00:28:05.957 --> 00:28:12.174
And at the time, my wife was working, coaching at the middle school.

376
00:28:12.174 --> 00:28:17.435
We put her in every little dance thing we could have.

377
00:28:17.435 --> 00:28:23.174
Dance school, in Sulphur, Lake Charles, took her all over the place.

378
00:28:23.174 --> 00:28:30.348
We even took her to . . . Drove all the way to Atlanta, Georgia for a contest.

379
00:28:30.348 --> 00:28:32.739
We put her in college.

380
00:28:32.739 --> 00:28:33.217
McNeese.

381
00:28:33.217 --> 00:28:36.565
And that's where I made a mistake.

382
00:28:36.565 --> 00:28:38.957
She's staying in a dorm.

383
00:28:38.957 --> 00:28:43.261
Freshman dorm with all the rest of the girls.

384
00:28:43.261 --> 00:28:47.087
Then we decide to buy her a car.

385
00:28:47.087 --> 00:28:50.435
Bought her a brand new little Dodge [?].

386
00:28:50.435 --> 00:28:53.783
And that's where we made a mistake.

387
00:28:53.783 --> 00:28:57.609
Her grades went to going down, down, down.

388
00:28:57.609 --> 00:29:06.217
And then she fell in love, so that's two years of college that was just shot to nothing.

389
00:29:06.217 --> 00:29:10.043
So she's married now and got three kids.

390
00:29:10.043 --> 00:29:11.000
When the

391
00:29:11.000 --> 00:29:15.783
<c.p>storm hit here, we had them two girls with us.

392
00:29:15.783 --> 00:29:22.000
Because when everybody got separated, our house got destroyed in Hurricane Rita.

393
00:29:22.000 --> 00:29:22.862
<v DRAGOON>What year?

394
00:29:22.862 --> 00:29:23.293
<v WILLIAMS>Rita.

395
00:29:23.293 --> 00:29:24.586
Got completely destroyed.

396
00:29:24.586 --> 00:29:35.362
So I was working at the sheriff's department at the time, so we had to take all the prisoners from Lake Charles to up north.

397
00:29:35.362 --> 00:29:37.086
To avoid the storm.

398
00:29:37.086 --> 00:29:40.103
And the storm tore Lake Charles up.

399
00:29:40.103 --> 00:29:43.552
So my wife and my family got separated.

400
00:29:43.552 --> 00:29:48.724
Because we had to stay at the jail when we came back.

401
00:29:48.724 --> 00:29:58.207
The next day after the storm had passed through, we had to bring the prisoners back, and we had to stay there.

402
00:29:58.207 --> 00:30:01.224
That was our home, all the deputies.

403
00:30:01.224 --> 00:30:05.103
Because everything else in Lake Charles was shut down.

404
00:30:05.103 --> 00:30:13.724
So finally when the school where they was, they had to go to the medical center hospital in Shreveport [Louisiana].

405
00:30:13.724 --> 00:30:19.328
And from Shreveport, they moved them back to Lafayette [Louisiana] in an apartment.

406
00:30:19.328 --> 00:30:26.655
So it took me about, I guess, over a week before I can discover where they were.

407
00:30:26.655 --> 00:30:30.103
So them two girls is still with us.

408
00:30:30.103 --> 00:30:37.000
Now they go with their parents on the weekends because they live in Lake Charles.

409
00:30:37.000 --> 00:30:46.420
And I had to get special . . . Because of my contact with the principals and the teachers . . . I have principals in Westlake that I coached,

410
00:30:46.420 --> 00:30:48.775
and a lot of the teachers.

411
00:30:48.775 --> 00:30:55.055
So because they been knowing me, I got special permission that let them go out alone.

412
00:30:55.055 --> 00:30:59.372
So all their schooling has been basically right there in Westlake.

413
00:30:59.372 --> 00:31:05.652
One is a middle school now, and the other one is fifth grade at Western Height.

414
00:31:05.652 --> 00:31:07.615
So they still with us.

415
00:31:07.615 --> 00:31:13.110
Her husband didn't have a job, and I'll show you how good God is.

416
00:31:13.110 --> 00:31:16.642
He finished school from . . . I think it was LaGrange.

417
00:31:16.642 --> 00:31:20.175
And I got him a job on at Sasol.

418
00:31:20.175 --> 00:31:22.138
And that's where he's working.

419
00:31:22.138 --> 00:31:25.670
And I also got him in the welding school.

420
00:31:25.670 --> 00:31:34.698
He wanted to go to welding school, and it just so happened again, I put God in this, the director of the welding

421
00:31:34.698 --> 00:31:37.052
<c.p>school, me and him coached together.

422
00:31:37.052 --> 00:31:43.332
So by him knowing me and I'm knowing him, he worked him in that welding class.

423
00:31:43.332 --> 00:31:49.612
So he got about . . . I think about six more months before he'll get finished, I believe.

424
00:31:49.612 --> 00:31:55.108
My daughter, she works at Sally's, the beauty place over there in Lake Charles.

425
00:31:55.108 --> 00:31:59.425
So she had this little boy which is four years old.

426
00:31:59.425 --> 00:32:04.135
Now both of them working, but the little boy lives with them.

427
00:32:04.135 --> 00:32:09.238
Well it just so happens, I pick up, I get [Sulphur?] at mid-day.

428
00:32:09.238 --> 00:32:15.518
She got a car, but her husband got to use the car to go to work.

429
00:32:15.518 --> 00:32:17.872
And she works on Ryan Street.

430
00:32:17.872 --> 00:32:25.722
So when I get off work at my job, I go pick her up, drop her off to her job.

431
00:32:25.722 --> 00:32:29.255
Then I bring the little boy home with me.

432
00:32:29.255 --> 00:32:32.002
So now he's a spoiled little rat.

433
00:32:32.002 --> 00:32:35.535
Everywhere I move, he wants to go with me.

434
00:32:35.535 --> 00:32:37.498
But he stay with them.

435
00:32:37.498 --> 00:32:42.208
You know, only time he stayed with us is when they're working.

436
00:32:42.208 --> 00:32:46.918
And they pick him up in the evening, but them other girls,

437
00:32:46.918 --> 00:32:51.628
they . . . I don’t think they really want to stay with their mama,

438
00:32:51.628 --> 00:32:55.945
but at some point they going . . . They’d rather stay with us.

439
00:32:55.945 --> 00:33:03.402
But a lot of time I want to blame my wife, say she never should've went got that baby.

440
00:33:03.402 --> 00:33:07.328
Because we had four kids, and all of them grown.

441
00:33:07.328 --> 00:33:10.860
And here we have to start all over again.

442
00:33:10.860 --> 00:33:14.000
So, that's where we are with that.

443
00:33:14.000 --> 00:33:14.437
<v DRAGOON>Now. . .

444
00:33:14.437 --> 00:33:19.238
<v WILLIAMS>So now we don’t want to depart from them either, but . . .

445
00:33:19.238 --> 00:33:24.040
<v DRAGOON>You mentioned you were six years old when you were adopted?

446
00:33:24.040 --> 00:33:24.476
<v WILLIAMS>Right.

447
00:33:24.476 --> 00:33:26.222
<v DRAGOON>Into the Williams family?

448
00:33:26.222 --> 00:33:26.659
<v WILLIAMS>Right.

449
00:33:26.659 --> 00:33:29.714
<v DRAGOON>How did your biological mother find them?

450
00:33:29.714 --> 00:33:31.460
How did that happen?

451
00:33:31.460 --> 00:33:38.444
<v WILLIAMS>Well see, my biological mother, at the time I was born, she was living in Houston.

452
00:33:38.444 --> 00:33:44.119
And really, I never did know, and don't know today, my biological daddy.

453
00:33:44.119 --> 00:33:45.865
I really don't care.

454
00:33:45.865 --> 00:33:48.048
I saw him one time.

455
00:33:48.048 --> 00:33:54.595
But she couldn't handle me over there in Houston because she was living bad herself.

456
00:33:54.595 --> 00:34:02.016
So that's why they went . . . some of the relatives went and got me and brought me here.

457
00:34:02.016 --> 00:34:04.635
And I been here ever since.

458
00:34:04.635 --> 00:34:05.508
But now . . .

459
00:34:05.508 --> 00:34:08.127
<v DRAGOON>Did she have relatives in Mossville?

460
00:34:08.127 --> 00:34:09.000
<v WILLIAMS>Who?

461
00:34:09.000 --> 00:34:10.565
<v DRAGOON>Your biological mother.

462
00:34:10.565 --> 00:34:16.826
<v WILLIAMS>My biological mother was raised by the same grandparents who raised me.

463
00:34:16.826 --> 00:34:17.348
<v DRAGOON>She . . .

464
00:34:17.348 --> 00:34:18.913
<v WILLIAMS>They raised her.

465
00:34:18.913 --> 00:34:21.000
<v DRAGOON>She was young?

466
00:34:21.000 --> 00:34:21.445
<v WILLIAMS>Yeah.

467
00:34:21.445 --> 00:34:24.115
And her brother and her sister.

468
00:34:24.115 --> 00:34:24.560
Both.

469
00:34:24.560 --> 00:34:25.005
<v DRAGOON>Wow.

470
00:34:25.005 --> 00:34:34.796
<v WILLIAMS>See my mama . . . My adopted mama, they got some people still living in Mossville said she always was that kind, bringing in.

471
00:34:34.796 --> 00:34:37.911
They never had kids of their own.

472
00:34:37.911 --> 00:34:40.581
She was always bringing somebody else.

473
00:34:40.581 --> 00:34:47.257
And I think that's where my wife got that from because we was staying there.

474
00:34:47.257 --> 00:34:49.482
Because she always bringing somebody.

475
00:34:49.482 --> 00:34:53.042
Cooking for them, taking care of they kids.

476
00:34:53.042 --> 00:34:54.377
And doing this.

477
00:34:54.377 --> 00:34:57.492
So that's how I got to Mossville.

478
00:34:57.492 --> 00:35:02.387
<v DRAGOON>What are your earliest memories of Mossville and the Williams family?

479
00:35:02.387 --> 00:35:05.503
Meeting them for the first time, or . . .

480
00:35:05.503 --> 00:35:07.283
<v WILLIAMS>Well see, my mama.

481
00:35:07.283 --> 00:35:10.398
My adopted mama, she always worked out.

482
00:35:10.398 --> 00:35:19.298
She worked for rich people like the . . . I can't remember all of them name now, but she cook for them.

483
00:35:19.298 --> 00:35:23.749
And not only that, she worked at the sulfur mines.

484
00:35:23.749 --> 00:35:32.649
And we always had a car, so she picked up ladies, and she was some little like supervisor, I guess.

485
00:35:32.649 --> 00:35:37.990
She picked up people in Mossville and brought them, give them jobs.

486
00:35:37.990 --> 00:35:39.770
But she work for [?].

487
00:35:39.770 --> 00:35:42.885
He's a big gambler in Lake Charles.

488
00:35:42.885 --> 00:35:44.220
<v DRAGOON>Who is that?

489
00:35:44.220 --> 00:35:45.110
<v WILLIAMS>[The Gumlys?]

490
00:35:45.110 --> 00:35:46.000
<v DRAGOON>Oh.

491
00:35:46.000 --> 00:35:50.005
<v WILLIAMS>They had restaurants, hotels, and they were gambling people.

492
00:35:50.005 --> 00:35:52.676
My mama always worked for them.

493
00:35:52.676 --> 00:35:59.352
She worked in the restaurant, and then she brought other people in there to work.

494
00:35:59.352 --> 00:36:00.687
Give them jobs.

495
00:36:00.687 --> 00:36:04.247
That's about all I can remember on that.

496
00:36:04.247 --> 00:36:11.813
Like my daddy had the wood yard, and she always work, and they always had a car.

497
00:36:11.813 --> 00:36:18.044
That's how I learned. . . I stole the car one day when they was gone.

498
00:36:18.044 --> 00:36:23.830
Had a big old field, and that's how I learned how to drive.

499
00:36:23.830 --> 00:36:24.720
<v DRAGOON>By yourself?

500
00:36:24.720 --> 00:36:25.165
<v WILLIAMS>Yeah.

501
00:36:25.165 --> 00:36:28.725
Two things I think about the other day.

502
00:36:28.725 --> 00:36:31.396
My daddy always would tell me.

503
00:36:31.396 --> 00:36:40.297
My mama whipped me so many times, I tell you what, if I had the dollars, I probably could retire.

504
00:36:40.297 --> 00:36:46.527
Because I was a . . . They say I was a bad little joker coming up.

505
00:36:46.527 --> 00:36:47.863
<v DRAGOON>You were bad?

506
00:36:47.863 --> 00:36:50.533
<v WILLIAMS>Well, really just getting into trouble.

507
00:36:50.533 --> 00:36:54.093
Doing things, hardheaded, I guess that's the way.

508
00:36:54.093 --> 00:36:59.879
Because he told me, he said, one day all my buddies was swimming.

509
00:36:59.879 --> 00:37:02.549
We didn't have no swimming pool.

510
00:37:02.549 --> 00:37:07.000
We'd go in these dynamite holes in the woods.

511
00:37:07.000 --> 00:37:08.364
<v DRAGOON>What's a dynamite hole?

512
00:37:08.364 --> 00:37:11.432
<v WILLIAMS>That's where they dig like these dirt pits today.

513
00:37:11.432 --> 00:37:13.136
Like they dig for dirt.

514
00:37:13.136 --> 00:37:15.864
And what it does, it becomes a pond.

515
00:37:15.864 --> 00:37:20.295
And we call it dynamite hole because that’s the way they dug it.

516
00:37:20.295 --> 00:37:26.091
They didn’t dig it with a machine, they dynamited it and made these holes for dirt purposes.

517
00:37:26.091 --> 00:37:28.818
And he told me not to go swimming.

518
00:37:28.818 --> 00:37:35.295
So I went and I sat on the bank looking at everybody, and I decided I'm getting in it.

519
00:37:35.295 --> 00:37:39.727
So I got in and that's where I learned how to swim, really.

520
00:37:39.727 --> 00:37:42.795
They taught me how to swim in that hole.

521
00:37:42.795 --> 00:37:49.955
But when I came home, I didn't have sense enough and all he had to do is look at my head.

522
00:37:49.955 --> 00:37:53.364
And that's when he whipped me one time for that.

523
00:37:53.364 --> 00:37:56.091
That's the only whipping he ever gave me.

524
00:37:56.091 --> 00:38:02.909
But my mama gave me so many whippings she'd tell me not to do, and I’d go do it anyway.

525
00:38:02.909 --> 00:38:04.955
You have to pay the price.

526
00:38:04.955 --> 00:38:07.000
But I'm glad she did.

527
00:38:07.000 --> 00:38:09.350
<v DRAGOON>So she did most of the disciplining?

528
00:38:09.350 --> 00:38:10.021
<v WILLIAMS>Yeah, definitely.

529
00:38:10.021 --> 00:38:18.749
Because a lot of time she get on, my daddy had to come in there and stop her because she didn't know when to stop whipping.

530
00:38:18.749 --> 00:38:22.442
See in them time, we had a lot of plum trees.

531
00:38:22.442 --> 00:38:30.498
And you know what a plum tree, I don’t know if you ever been whipped by a plum tree, but them switches is tough.

532
00:38:30.498 --> 00:38:39.226
And she sent me out there to go get it, and I get the smallest one knowing she's going to get to go get another one.

533
00:38:39.226 --> 00:38:41.576
And she didn't know when to stop.

534
00:38:41.576 --> 00:38:45.604
And he had to come in there many times and stop her,

535
00:38:45.604 --> 00:38:51.647
but I could kiss her grave today for them whippings she gave me because I never been arrested.

536
00:38:51.647 --> 00:38:56.346
Only time I been in jail is when I went there to work [Laughs].

537
00:38:56.346 --> 00:38:58.360
But she was a strong disciplinarian.

538
00:38:58.360 --> 00:39:01.382
But my dad, he did a lot of talking.

539
00:39:01.382 --> 00:39:06.417
But the worst whipping, I don't know if you want to put this in there.

540
00:39:06.417 --> 00:39:10.110
The worst whipping she gave me has stuck with me today.

541
00:39:10.110 --> 00:39:15.481
My daddy always used to tell me this, he’d say, "Boy . . ." He give me an allowance.

542
00:39:15.481 --> 00:39:17.830
Every week I got that twenty-five cent.

543
00:39:17.830 --> 00:39:18.502
On Saturday.

544
00:39:18.502 --> 00:39:19.173
Twenty-five cent

545
00:39:19.173 --> 00:39:20.516
<c.p>meant a whole lot.

546
00:39:20.516 --> 00:39:24.544
You could take a nickel and buy a whole lot with it.

547
00:39:24.544 --> 00:39:26.558
He said, "You going out here?"

548
00:39:26.558 --> 00:39:30.922
And that's when I was go out at night, sixteen, seventeen years old.

549
00:39:30.922 --> 00:39:36.965
He said, "If you go get in trouble, I'm going to come see about you for one thing."

550
00:39:36.965 --> 00:39:39.650
Said, “If you steal something, I ain't coming.

551
00:39:39.650 --> 00:39:42.000
You going to stay in there."

552
00:39:42.000 --> 00:39:44.695
So anyway, growing up, we got teenagers.

553
00:39:44.695 --> 00:39:50.855
They had . . . only grocery store they had in Mossville was right down there by the track.

554
00:39:50.855 --> 00:39:51.625
Ira Garrett.

555
00:39:51.625 --> 00:39:56.245
And he, in Mossville, was compared to be like the wealthy man.

556
00:39:56.245 --> 00:40:01.251
Because he trapped animals and all that, raccoons, and he sold them hides.

557
00:40:01.251 --> 00:40:07.796
But he had that grocery store where everybody . . . That was something like the little Wal-Mart . . . not Wal-Mart.

558
00:40:07.796 --> 00:40:09.336
Market Basket for Mossville.

559
00:40:09.336 --> 00:40:12.031
And he raised a lot of chickens.

560
00:40:12.031 --> 00:40:16.266
Them chickens running all around in the woods and all around.

561
00:40:16.266 --> 00:40:18.576
Because this is a wooded area.

562
00:40:18.576 --> 00:40:21.656
So my little gang, we had BB guns.

563
00:40:21.656 --> 00:40:24.736
They’d buy you a BB gun for Christmas.

564
00:40:24.736 --> 00:40:29.357
So they had a rice field right down by the railroad tracks.

565
00:40:29.357 --> 00:40:32.822
Down there, see where the railroad is right here?

566
00:40:32.822 --> 00:40:36.287
They had a big old rice field down there.

567
00:40:36.287 --> 00:40:38.597
Well, they had these marsh hens.

568
00:40:38.597 --> 00:40:40.522
They call them marsh hens.

569
00:40:40.522 --> 00:40:43.602
These birds that stay in the rice field.

570
00:40:43.602 --> 00:40:47.452
And we go down there and try to shoot them.

571
00:40:47.452 --> 00:40:49.377
Because you could eat them.

572
00:40:49.377 --> 00:40:53.227
So we decide one day we wasn't going do that.

573
00:40:53.227 --> 00:40:56.693
We went and stole some of that man's chickens.

574
00:40:56.693 --> 00:41:00.543
And they wasn't but about that big, like little pullets.

575
00:41:00.543 --> 00:41:05.163
So we brought them to a friend's house right down the street.

576
00:41:05.163 --> 00:41:10.938
We was going to cut us a hole, we was going to have a barbecue.

577
00:41:10.938 --> 00:41:17.868
What we did, we skinned the skin off of the chickens so they couldn't tell they were chickens.

578
00:41:17.868 --> 00:41:20.178
We told they were marsh hens.

579
00:41:20.178 --> 00:41:27.879
Now we didn't have no telephones at that time that I can remember, but we had our little feast cooked.

580
00:41:27.879 --> 00:41:34.039
When I got home, my mama had the . . . She done got the word what we did.

581
00:41:34.039 --> 00:41:37.889
And boy, she got me out under that oak tree.

582
00:41:37.889 --> 00:41:40.199
We had a big old tree.

583
00:41:40.199 --> 00:41:47.129
It was so big that you could put cars under and use that as kind of a garage.

584
00:41:47.129 --> 00:41:49.054
She got me under there

585
00:41:49.054 --> 00:41:54.059
<c.p>and she got the Bible and she started in the book of Proverbs.

586
00:41:54.059 --> 00:41:59.450
And that's the worst whipping I ever had because she worked on my mind.

587
00:41:59.450 --> 00:41:59.835
Yeah.

588
00:41:59.835 --> 00:42:07.535
Because that . . . She could have took that switch and maybe thirty minutes I'd stop hurting, but that stuck with me.

589
00:42:07.535 --> 00:42:11.000
Like I say, she was a good disciplinarian.

590
00:42:11.000 --> 00:42:14.643
<v DRAGOON>What else do you remember about your parents?

591
00:42:14.643 --> 00:42:18.741
<v WILLIAMS>Well, my daddy, they say he used to gamble.

592
00:42:18.741 --> 00:42:24.661
But when I got of age, I never did see any of that.

593
00:42:24.661 --> 00:42:27.393
He didn't smoke, he didn't drink.

594
00:42:27.393 --> 00:42:35.134
If he did, I never, and I can vouch for it, I never seen, he never did.

595
00:42:35.134 --> 00:42:37.866
But my mama, she would smoke.

596
00:42:37.866 --> 00:42:41.509
She smoked and drank coffee all day long.

597
00:42:41.509 --> 00:42:47.884
And she would always go, make sure we go to church on Sunday now.

598
00:42:47.884 --> 00:42:56.991
You could go out on Saturday night, but come Sunday morning, you going to get up and go to church.

599
00:42:56.991 --> 00:43:00.634
But my dad, it was a long time.

600
00:43:00.634 --> 00:43:06.554
I think he got down sick before he decided to join the church.

601
00:43:06.554 --> 00:43:09.741
But he was just a hardworking man.

602
00:43:09.741 --> 00:43:13.839
But you looking at, as I see it now,

603
00:43:13.839 --> 00:43:22.946
you looking because I think my dad only had about fifth grade education, and also my mom about fifth grade.

604
00:43:22.946 --> 00:43:25.679
But they had good common sense.

605
00:43:25.679 --> 00:43:30.232
<v DRAGOON>Where did they . . . Did they grow up in Mossville too?

606
00:43:30.232 --> 00:43:32.964
<v WILLIAMS>No, she came from Colfax, Louisiana.

607
00:43:32.964 --> 00:43:36.607
That's somewhere on the other side of Lafayette.

608
00:43:36.607 --> 00:43:44.348
Now, my daddy and them, they came here when . . . I don’t know exactly where they came from.

609
00:43:44.348 --> 00:43:48.902
But I think it was somewhere around DeRidder [Louisiana] area.

610
00:43:48.902 --> 00:43:51.179
And they came here sharecroppers.

611
00:43:51.179 --> 00:43:53.000
<v DRAGOON>They came here?

612
00:43:53.000 --> 00:43:54.022
<v WILLIAMS>Yeah, sharecroppers.

613
00:43:54.022 --> 00:43:54.533
<v DRAGOON>Okay.

614
00:43:54.533 --> 00:43:58.620
<v WILLIAMS>And that's how they got all that land.

615
00:43:58.620 --> 00:44:00.153
Called Williams Head.

616
00:44:00.153 --> 00:44:05.773
Williams Head about two hundred acres of land at one time.

617
00:44:05.773 --> 00:44:12.926
The Gulf State bought some of it, the plants bought what they had left.

618
00:44:12.926 --> 00:44:14.969
But . . . they were sharecroppers.

619
00:44:14.969 --> 00:44:18.546
I'm thinking what else they call that.

620
00:44:18.546 --> 00:44:22.633
They had a lot of lumber around here.

621
00:44:22.633 --> 00:44:24.166
They had sawmills.

622
00:44:24.166 --> 00:44:27.231
You know what a sawmill is?

623
00:44:27.231 --> 00:44:29.786
Because I worked at sawmills.

624
00:44:29.786 --> 00:44:39.493
That's where they cut logs and they run them through a machine and make lumber. [someone coughs] Bless you.

625
00:44:39.493 --> 00:44:40.515
Make lumber.

626
00:44:40.515 --> 00:44:48.179
And they ship that lumber out and they used to float it down the river.

627
00:44:48.179 --> 00:44:51.755
And they had another wood yard, [?] Rigmaiden.

628
00:44:51.755 --> 00:44:58.397
That's the only two wood yards that I knew they had in Mossville.

629
00:44:58.397 --> 00:45:04.017
And I could say they had that grocery store in Mossville.

630
00:45:04.017 --> 00:45:06.061
Had a post office.

631
00:45:06.061 --> 00:45:10.148
It was down there by the railroad track.

632
00:45:10.148 --> 00:45:14.747
And the other Rigmaiden, he had a cotton field.

633
00:45:14.747 --> 00:45:19.856
You know, cotton was real popular back in those days.

634
00:45:19.856 --> 00:45:26.498
And he'd pay us little boys to go out there and pick cotton.

635
00:45:26.498 --> 00:45:32.118
And that’s why I could never get a sack of cotton.

636
00:45:32.118 --> 00:45:34.162
I pick, pick, pick.

637
00:45:34.162 --> 00:45:37.227
And that was the other business.

638
00:45:37.227 --> 00:45:41.314
And then later, Mossville got its first drugstore.

639
00:45:41.314 --> 00:45:50.000
That's [Purdy?] drug store, and that drug store was right down there where . . . past the school.

640
00:45:50.000 --> 00:45:53.773
<v DRAGOON>Do you remember when that was built?

641
00:45:53.773 --> 00:45:58.625
<v WILLIAMS>No, I think it was somewhere in the sixties.

642
00:45:58.625 --> 00:46:00.242
The early sixties.

643
00:46:00.242 --> 00:46:00.781
And . . .

644
00:46:00.781 --> 00:46:02.398
<v DRAGOON>What . . . Oh, sorry.

645
00:46:02.398 --> 00:46:08.328
<v WILLIAMS>The passenger train used to stop in Mossville right down there.

646
00:46:08.328 --> 00:46:09.945
By the store.

647
00:46:09.945 --> 00:46:25.039
And people would get on the train and go to . . . I guess they’d go to New Orleans because I think that train came all the way to California.

648
00:46:25.039 --> 00:46:25.578
KCS.

649
00:46:25.578 --> 00:46:31.508
And people would catch the train there and they’d get off.

650
00:46:31.508 --> 00:46:33.664
Like a little depot.

651
00:46:33.664 --> 00:46:36.359
They wouldn't stay there long.

652
00:46:36.359 --> 00:46:40.672
About ten minutes you'd be, they was moving.

653
00:46:40.672 --> 00:46:43.906
And let's see what other business.

654
00:46:43.906 --> 00:46:44.445
Well . . .

655
00:46:44.445 --> 00:46:48.758
<v DRAGOON>How long was the passenger train in operation?

656
00:46:48.758 --> 00:46:56.844
<v WILLIAMS>I guess the passenger train stopped running maybe in the late . . . yeah, about ’59 . . . ‘57.

657
00:46:56.844 --> 00:46:59.000
Somewhere in there.

658
00:46:59.000 --> 00:47:00.719
<v DRAGOON>Did you ever take it?

659
00:47:00.719 --> 00:47:06.906
<v WILLIAMS>No, but we'd be out there all the time watching because we used to go down that track.

660
00:47:06.906 --> 00:47:10.000
We'd be scared, they'd go down there and crawfish.

661
00:47:10.000 --> 00:47:16.875
And boy, when you hear that train coming we'd hit crossing the woods, see, because we was afraid of it.

662
00:47:16.875 --> 00:47:21.344
Well I think that one stopped running when they built that other railroad.

663
00:47:21.344 --> 00:47:27.188
See the one that you see now that go all the way through from east to west.

664
00:47:27.188 --> 00:47:28.906
They didn't have that one.

665
00:47:28.906 --> 00:47:32.344
The only one they had was this one right here.

666
00:47:32.344 --> 00:47:37.844
Well this still here, but they use it now as a . . . I guess for the plants.

667
00:47:37.844 --> 00:47:42.312
But that track has been there, oh, as long as I can remember.

668
00:47:42.312 --> 00:47:45.750
And I'm trying to think of something else about . . . Well,

669
00:47:45.750 --> 00:47:50.562
out here I saw the industry grow because they didn’t have but one plant . . .

670
00:47:50.562 --> 00:47:51.250
<v DRAGOON>Which plant?

671
00:47:51.250 --> 00:47:52.625
<v WILLIAMS>. . . when I grew up.

672
00:47:52.625 --> 00:47:54.000
That was Conoco.

673
00:47:54.000 --> 00:47:54.520
<v DRAGOON>Okay.

674
00:47:54.520 --> 00:47:55.040
<v DRAGOON>Okay.

675
00:47:55.040 --> 00:47:56.599
When did they . . .

676
00:47:56.599 --> 00:47:59.198
<v WILLIAMS>It was known as Pumpelly.

677
00:47:59.198 --> 00:48:01.277
And it was Pumpelly.

678
00:48:01.277 --> 00:48:01.797
<v DRAGOON>Pumpelly?

679
00:48:01.797 --> 00:48:02.317
<v WILLIAMS>Yeah.

680
00:48:02.317 --> 00:48:05.436
And then it grew into Conoco.

681
00:48:05.436 --> 00:48:15.312
And then over on [Highway] 90, where you see the interstate now, they had another plant that was Madison.

682
00:48:15.312 --> 00:48:21.030
Okay, and it would go west of here, they had Firestone.

683
00:48:21.030 --> 00:48:32.985
Now that's the only three plants that I can remember growing up: Conoco, Olin Madison, which they changed the name now, and Firestone.

684
00:48:32.985 --> 00:48:38.703
So all these other plants you see come in after that.

685
00:48:38.703 --> 00:48:41.822
But I saw all of them.

686
00:48:41.822 --> 00:48:44.941
All my uncles retired from plants.

687
00:48:44.941 --> 00:48:48.059
I never worked in a plant.

688
00:48:48.059 --> 00:48:50.658
I worked in a sawmill.

689
00:48:50.658 --> 00:48:54.817
But all my uncles retired from the plant.

690
00:48:54.817 --> 00:49:04.173
And my oldest son now, my oldest son, he graduated from Westlake High School while I was there.

691
00:49:04.173 --> 00:49:08.332
Matter of fact, he ran track for me.

692
00:49:08.332 --> 00:49:16.129
Well my uncle was a supervisor at Olin, and he got him on at Olin.

693
00:49:16.129 --> 00:49:22.366
And he went to SOWELA Tech . . . well, he was going for instrumentation.

694
00:49:22.366 --> 00:49:24.446
He retired from Olin.

695
00:49:24.446 --> 00:49:30.163
Now he's married now, and his wife is an x-ray technician.

696
00:49:30.163 --> 00:49:39.000
She works at . . . between all the hospitals in Lake Charles, I think, Memorial and St. Pat.

697
00:49:39.000 --> 00:49:42.313
<c.p>And then . . . But my son retired from Olin.

698
00:49:42.313 --> 00:49:46.868
And now he went to work for Westlake Parliament in instrumentation.

699
00:49:46.868 --> 00:49:51.009
And he's been . . . I guess this is thirty-some years now.

700
00:49:51.009 --> 00:49:56.393
So he's the only one that, out of my family, worked in industry.

701
00:49:56.393 --> 00:50:01.776
My other son, my youngest son, he graduated Southern University [Baton Rouge, LA].

702
00:50:01.776 --> 00:50:03.847
He teaches in Houston [Texas].

703
00:50:03.847 --> 00:50:09.230
My middle son, he's at home because he's had two heart attacks now.

704
00:50:09.230 --> 00:50:10.887
He graduated from Sulphur.

705
00:50:10.887 --> 00:50:15.028
He was an all-state running back at Sulphur High School.

706
00:50:15.028 --> 00:50:18.340
You want to hear this story about him?

707
00:50:18.340 --> 00:50:22.896
He had . . . When he graduated, I mean he was all-state tailback.

708
00:50:22.896 --> 00:50:23.724
Sulphur High.

709
00:50:23.724 --> 00:50:24.552
So far.

710
00:50:24.552 --> 00:50:28.693
He had a scholarship to go to McNeese play football.

711
00:50:28.693 --> 00:50:34.491
The dean called me at Westlake and said, "Coach," he say, "Where's your son?"

712
00:50:34.491 --> 00:50:36.147
I say, "He's home."

713
00:50:36.147 --> 00:50:42.773
He said, "Well, he was supposed to report today," and he say he didn't show up.

714
00:50:42.773 --> 00:50:44.015
I say, "What?"

715
00:50:44.015 --> 00:50:53.126
So I went to the principal's office and have somebody to cover my class, and I went home and I found him.

716
00:50:53.126 --> 00:50:58.509
And I said, "Boy, why you . . ." He said, "Daddy I'm tired of football.

717
00:50:58.509 --> 00:51:01.408
I played pee-wee football, junior high football."

718
00:51:01.408 --> 00:51:04.721
He say, "I want to get a job."

719
00:51:04.721 --> 00:51:11.761
I say, "Well that's where you going to make a living, you going to get a job."

720
00:51:11.761 --> 00:51:13.003
He said, "Yeah."

721
00:51:13.003 --> 00:51:14.660
I say, "Okay then."

722
00:51:14.660 --> 00:51:19.629
So what I did, [Shooky Miles?], white guy that was over R.E.

723
00:51:19.629 --> 00:51:20.457
Heidt Construction.

724
00:51:20.457 --> 00:51:22.113
He was a superintendent.

725
00:51:22.113 --> 00:51:25.840
And he was in our booster club at Westlake.

726
00:51:25.840 --> 00:51:28.739
So I told him about my son.

727
00:51:28.739 --> 00:51:33.709
I said, "See if you can get him a job in construction."

728
00:51:33.709 --> 00:51:38.264
He say, "Well, yeah," he said, "I'll get him a job."

729
00:51:38.264 --> 00:51:44.475
He said, "You tell him to go to the labor hall and get his card."

730
00:51:44.475 --> 00:51:47.374
Which I think was about fifty dollars.

731
00:51:47.374 --> 00:51:54.000
I gave fifty dollars, he went there and hooked him up and got that card.

732
00:51:54.000 --> 00:51:54.714
<v DRAGOON>Which card?

733
00:51:54.714 --> 00:51:58.282
<v WILLIAMS>It's a labor card that you can work in plants.

734
00:51:58.282 --> 00:52:00.423
See, they still use that, you

735
00:52:00.423 --> 00:52:02.207
<c.p>got to have that card.

736
00:52:02.207 --> 00:52:09.700
You got to go get a physical, and then you go to the labor union and you pay for that card.

737
00:52:09.700 --> 00:52:13.982
And that's just like an admit that you get in the plant.

738
00:52:13.982 --> 00:52:14.696
ID like.

739
00:52:14.696 --> 00:52:21.119
So he gave him a job, I said, "Now Shook, I want you to do this for me.

740
00:52:21.119 --> 00:52:23.260
Put him on the hardest thing."

741
00:52:23.260 --> 00:52:25.044
I said, "Don't kill him.

742
00:52:25.044 --> 00:52:27.899
Put him on the hardest thing you got."

743
00:52:27.899 --> 00:52:29.326
So he did that.

744
00:52:29.326 --> 00:52:31.110
He worked about six months.

745
00:52:31.110 --> 00:52:32.894
And I saw Shook again.

746
00:52:32.894 --> 00:52:35.035
We met up at the restaurant.

747
00:52:35.035 --> 00:52:38.247
Shooky said, "Coach, I think he wants to quit.

748
00:52:38.247 --> 00:52:40.388
But he's got too much pride.

749
00:52:40.388 --> 00:52:44.670
See, he asked me when there going to be the next lay-off."

750
00:52:44.670 --> 00:52:47.167
I said, "Shooky, put it to him."

751
00:52:47.167 --> 00:52:49.665
So really, they did have another lay-off.

752
00:52:49.665 --> 00:52:51.806
And he got caught in it.

753
00:52:51.806 --> 00:52:54.661
And he stayed off drawing, what is that.

754
00:52:54.661 --> 00:52:55.018
Unemployment.

755
00:52:55.018 --> 00:52:59.656
So I said, "Shooky," again I said, "Shooky, you all start back high?"

756
00:52:59.656 --> 00:53:01.084
And he said, "Yeah.

757
00:53:01.084 --> 00:53:02.511
Your boy ain't working?"

758
00:53:02.511 --> 00:53:06.436
Said, "No." Said, "Tell him to come by in the morning.

759
00:53:06.436 --> 00:53:08.577
Say I got something for him."

760
00:53:08.577 --> 00:53:13.573
I told him and he said, "No, Daddy, I want to go to school.

761
00:53:13.573 --> 00:53:15.000
I'm going to school."

762
00:53:15.000 --> 00:53:20.352
So that's . . . They put him in some kind of school to work for Gulf State.

763
00:53:20.352 --> 00:53:23.564
And he worked for Gulf State for twelve years.

764
00:53:23.564 --> 00:53:25.705
Reading meters all over this area.

765
00:53:25.705 --> 00:53:29.987
He was make . . . his salary was the equivalent to a beginning teacher.

766
00:53:29.987 --> 00:53:32.485
Boy bought him brand a new Vette.

767
00:53:32.485 --> 00:53:33.912
Still staying with us.

768
00:53:33.912 --> 00:53:38.194
So finally, when Entergy merged with Gulf State, they cut their salaries.

769
00:53:38.194 --> 00:53:46.758
See, well instead of him talking to me, he got [into the buddies?] and all say, "Well we going draw down all our money.

770
00:53:46.758 --> 00:53:48.185
We going to quit."

771
00:53:48.185 --> 00:53:50.683
And that's where he made the mistake.

772
00:53:50.683 --> 00:53:55.322
See, he should have stayed there with Entergy and worked himself back up.

773
00:53:55.322 --> 00:53:59.960
And so since then, he had two heart attacks trying to work construction.

774
00:53:59.960 --> 00:54:01.744
He can't work no more.

775
00:54:01.744 --> 00:54:05.670
He's still at home, but he's, I think, is a blessing.

776
00:54:05.670 --> 00:54:08.524
Because he helps out all around the house.

777
00:54:08.524 --> 00:54:12.806
He cuts the yard and does all the things for his mama.

778
00:54:12.806 --> 00:54:14.947
Anything they need him to do.

779
00:54:14.947 --> 00:54:17.088
Well his sister got him spoiled.

780
00:54:17.088 --> 00:54:21.727
She's still a registered nurse, and she pays him for cleaning her car.

781
00:54:21.727 --> 00:54:23.511
And she has a garden.

782
00:54:23.511 --> 00:54:26.366
Anything that she’s putting money in his pocket.

783
00:54:26.366 --> 00:54:28.863
But right now he's applying for disability.

784
00:54:28.863 --> 00:54:31.004
I think he might get it.

785
00:54:31.004 --> 00:54:32.075
This is his

786
00:54:32.075 --> 00:54:36.000
<c.p>second time, because they turned him down the first time.

787
00:54:36.000 --> 00:54:40.242
<v DRAGOON>What are the names of your children?

788
00:54:40.242 --> 00:54:41.455
<v WILLIAMS>The names?

789
00:54:41.455 --> 00:54:45.091
The oldest one is LaSalle, Junior.

790
00:54:45.091 --> 00:54:49.939
Then the next one is Arthur Troy Williams.

791
00:54:49.939 --> 00:54:53.576
And then they got Ephraim Williams.

792
00:54:53.576 --> 00:54:59.636
That's the one that teach the boys and the girls.

793
00:54:59.636 --> 00:55:02.667
The nurse, she's Glinda Williams.

794
00:55:02.667 --> 00:55:06.909
And the one we adopted is Lakeisha.

795
00:55:06.909 --> 00:55:16.000
But she was [Gwaltney?], but her married name now, she's a [Boyger?] . . . Lakeisha Boyger.

796
00:55:16.000 --> 00:55:27.604
<v DRAGOON>What stories did your parents tell you about the early history of Mossville when they first came, and do you remember what year that was?

797
00:55:27.604 --> 00:55:29.925
<v WILLIAMS>Must have been in ‘32.

798
00:55:29.925 --> 00:55:32.709
What year they had the . . . yeah.

799
00:55:32.709 --> 00:55:33.638
Been ‘32.

800
00:55:33.638 --> 00:55:34.102
1932.

801
00:55:34.102 --> 00:55:37.815
<v DRAGOON>Did they tell stories about the Depression era?

802
00:55:37.815 --> 00:55:41.064
<v WILLIAMS>That's when they was in the Depression.

803
00:55:41.064 --> 00:55:41.528
Yeah.

804
00:55:41.528 --> 00:55:46.634
Most talk was done then because that's when World War Two,

805
00:55:46.634 --> 00:55:58.702
and as I can remember a lot of talk about that because people had these fallout shelters and all that, and it's a lot of fear.

806
00:55:58.702 --> 00:56:04.272
But far as the history of Mossville, I grew up in it.

807
00:56:04.272 --> 00:56:13.091
Wasn't too much they could tell me except what they did growing up and how they made a living.

808
00:56:13.091 --> 00:56:16.340
Well, I saw that on my own.

809
00:56:16.340 --> 00:56:18.196
I went through that.

810
00:56:18.196 --> 00:56:26.087
Because I always try to . . . Like I said, my parents always gave me some money every week.

811
00:56:26.087 --> 00:56:28.408
But I didn't want that.

812
00:56:28.408 --> 00:56:33.513
I got big enough I wanted to earn on my own.

813
00:56:33.513 --> 00:56:37.226
So every summer school was out, we worked

814
00:56:37.226 --> 00:56:46.045
<c.p>in sawmill, rice field, pick cotton, and then I got a job at . . . White guy gave me a job.

815
00:56:46.045 --> 00:56:48.366
They had a filling station.

816
00:56:48.366 --> 00:56:49.294
Nice guy.

817
00:56:49.294 --> 00:56:53.936
He was a German, on Shelby Drive in Lake Charles.

818
00:56:53.936 --> 00:56:57.185
That's over there where the civic center.

819
00:56:57.185 --> 00:57:05.540
And every summer, he gave me a job at the station, washing cars, cleaning them, and changing oil.

820
00:57:05.540 --> 00:57:13.894
And on weekends, while I was going to school, high school, he give me job working on weekends.

821
00:57:13.894 --> 00:57:19.000
So I always kind of made a living for myself.

822
00:57:19.000 --> 00:57:24.777
And then I went to courting my wife, which was my high school sweetheart.

823
00:57:24.777 --> 00:57:27.665
We went to the same high school.

824
00:57:27.665 --> 00:57:31.791
She lived in Lake Charles, and I lived over here.

825
00:57:31.791 --> 00:57:35.917
Well, because I always worked, I always dressed pretty good.

826
00:57:35.917 --> 00:57:39.631
The kids thought I came from a rich family.

827
00:57:39.631 --> 00:57:40.869
But I ain't.

828
00:57:40.869 --> 00:57:49.947
And my first big paycheck, I went and bought me a suit from, they called it a credit clothing on Ryan Street.

829
00:57:49.947 --> 00:57:53.660
Y'all don't know nothing about Ryan, I don't believe.

830
00:57:53.660 --> 00:57:55.723
This was a credit clothing.

831
00:57:55.723 --> 00:58:01.500
I went and bought me a suit, hat, shirt and all that to match.

832
00:58:01.500 --> 00:58:07.689
And I went to Lake Charles, and I brought her to the movie that evening.

833
00:58:07.689 --> 00:58:10.578
Went to the movie on Railway Avenue.

834
00:58:10.578 --> 00:58:20.068
And so on that night, when I walked her back home, I had to come and catch a ride from the bus station.

835
00:58:20.068 --> 00:58:27.083
And I'm walking down Railway Avenue, and came a big rain, and I got caught in it.

836
00:58:27.083 --> 00:58:32.859
And just to show you, that little suit drawed up, my hat rim, everything.

837
00:58:32.859 --> 00:58:34.097
I got wet.

838
00:58:34.097 --> 00:58:39.874
I couldn’t wear that suit no more because it was a cheap suit [Laughs].

839
00:58:39.874 --> 00:58:44.000
But that’s some things I never should forget [Laughs].

840
00:58:44.000 --> 00:58:48.871
<v DRAGOON>Did you mostly buy clothes, or did your family make clothes?

841
00:58:48.871 --> 00:58:50.643
Did your mother sew?

842
00:58:50.643 --> 00:58:58.171
<v WILLIAMS>Well, Mother used to make shirts out of sacks . . . like flour sacks, rice sacks, what they buying.

843
00:58:58.171 --> 00:59:00.829
They take that and make clothes.

844
00:59:00.829 --> 00:59:03.929
Matter of fact, all the spreads and

845
00:59:03.929 --> 00:59:15.000
<c.p>stuff, they would meet at one another house, and I can remember they had in the room, big room, they had a big old table.

846
00:59:15.000 --> 00:59:19.871
And I don't know how they do, I could see it,

847
00:59:19.871 --> 00:59:27.400
but they all get around and they stretch that out, and that's where they make they quilts.

848
00:59:27.400 --> 00:59:30.057
They make quilts and all that.

849
00:59:30.057 --> 00:59:33.600
And that was a part of they recreation.

850
00:59:33.600 --> 00:59:36.257
But they use that for cover.

851
00:59:36.257 --> 00:59:40.243
Everything just about that we use, they made it.

852
00:59:40.243 --> 00:59:40.686
See.

853
00:59:40.686 --> 00:59:45.557
But when I got old enough, I bought all my clothes.

854
00:59:45.557 --> 00:59:54.857
Matter of fact, I had credit set up and two men, they had a [Gauge's?], they had a Wilson's . . . George Wilson.

855
00:59:54.857 --> 00:59:58.843
I don't know if you ever heard of that.

856
00:59:58.843 --> 01:00:05.929
But see, you go in there and buy, and then you pay so much each month.

857
01:00:05.929 --> 01:00:20.100
So I stayed dressed all the time because I worked and I took care of my . . . And I did that because I didn't want my parents to keep giving me that money,

858
01:00:20.100 --> 01:00:23.200
because I like to earn my own.

859
01:00:23.200 --> 01:00:27.186
I'm still that way; I haven't changed at all.

860
01:00:27.186 --> 01:00:30.729
That's why I retired and got another job.

861
01:00:30.729 --> 01:00:36.929
I like to do my own thing with my . . . But believe it or not,

862
01:00:36.929 --> 01:00:43.129
me and my wife been married, this will be, what . . . sixty . . . I think sixty-something.

863
01:00:43.129 --> 01:00:45.786
She going be mad at me.

864
01:00:45.786 --> 01:00:48.000
Be sixty-four, I think.

865
01:00:48.000 --> 01:00:51.381
<v DRAGOON>How old were you when you got married?

866
01:00:51.381 --> 01:00:54.340
<v WILLIAMS>I was twenty, and she was eighteen.

867
01:00:54.340 --> 01:00:59.835
The reason why I did that because we courted all through high school.

868
01:00:59.835 --> 01:01:03.639
But they had some more boys wanted her, too.

869
01:01:03.639 --> 01:01:07.443
They lived in Lake Charles, I lived over here.

870
01:01:07.443 --> 01:01:14.629
So when the military called me, I wasn't going to leave her here, so I married her.

871
01:01:14.629 --> 01:01:18.433
So I made sure nobody else got her [Laughs].

872
01:01:18.433 --> 01:01:20.969
And we been together ever since.

873
01:01:20.969 --> 01:01:25.196
So I guess God must have made her for me.

874
01:01:25.196 --> 01:01:27.309
<v DRAGOON>And she's from Lake Charles?

875
01:01:27.309 --> 01:01:27.732
<v WILLIAMS>Yeah.

876
01:01:27.732 --> 01:01:29.000
<v DRAGOON>Ms. Betty?

877
01:01:29.000 --> 01:01:29.526
<v WILLIAMS>Yeah.

878
01:01:29.526 --> 01:01:32.156
She's originally from Lake Charles.

879
01:01:32.156 --> 01:01:32.682
Right.

880
01:01:32.682 --> 01:01:35.313
Anything else on the plants?

881
01:01:35.313 --> 01:01:39.521
<v DRAGOON>What was it like seeing the refineries grow?

882
01:01:39.521 --> 01:01:43.730
<v WILLIAMS>Well, really at first, it didn't bother us.

883
01:01:43.730 --> 01:01:51.095
But when they start having all this environmental and explosions and fires and stuff,

884
01:01:51.095 --> 01:02:02.668
that's just when the plants start extending and developing new, I guess, new resources and all that, that’s when it became concern.

885
01:02:02.668 --> 01:02:06.351
Before, it didn't bother us at all.

886
01:02:06.351 --> 01:02:16.346
And they had different smells and odors and people begin to panic with the alarm that it cause cancer.

887
01:02:16.346 --> 01:02:18.976
But I do know this.

888
01:02:18.976 --> 01:02:28.972
All around this area we grew up, all in the woods, they had fruit trees: wild plums, grapes, muscadines.

889
01:02:28.972 --> 01:02:32.654
Y'all don't know nothing about those, huh?

890
01:02:32.654 --> 01:02:39.493
See, and you could go in the woods and pick fruit and pears.

891
01:02:39.493 --> 01:02:45.280
But when the plants came in there, all them trees died.

892
01:02:45.280 --> 01:02:47.384
Every one of them.

893
01:02:47.384 --> 01:02:52.118
They used to have what they called hickory nuts.

894
01:02:52.118 --> 01:02:58.957
Now these nuts you buy like in the . . . They call them English walnuts.

895
01:02:58.957 --> 01:03:04.218
They had them trees all over the place in Mossville.

896
01:03:04.218 --> 01:03:07.374
But you don't see them now.

897
01:03:07.374 --> 01:03:08.953
They all gone.

898
01:03:08.953 --> 01:03:16.318
So they contribute, they say the plants, whatever chemicals they putting in the air.

899
01:03:16.318 --> 01:03:20.000
So we saw all of that.

900
01:03:20.000 --> 01:03:25.196
<v DRAGOON>Did folks know that it was because of the plants that they were disappearing?

901
01:03:25.196 --> 01:03:25.567
<v WILLIAMS>Yeah.

902
01:03:25.567 --> 01:03:30.763
Was nothing you could do about it, because you didn't have no resources then.

903
01:03:30.763 --> 01:03:33.732
Like they have EPA and all these people.

904
01:03:33.732 --> 01:03:35.588
They didn’t have that then.

905
01:03:35.588 --> 01:03:40.412
These plants did whatever they want to, that's why they polluted the place.

906
01:03:40.412 --> 01:03:49.320
See like a place over there where they call Bayou D’Inde, we used to go down there and catch the biggest perch as kids.

907
01:03:49.320 --> 01:03:53.773
Dig up some worms, and go out there with a fishing pole.

908
01:03:53.773 --> 01:03:56.371
All of a sudden, it become dead.

909
01:03:56.371 --> 01:03:58.227
And it's still dead today.

910
01:03:58.227 --> 01:04:04.536
Trying to reactivate it, whatever, but it'll never be the same because them plants had polluted it.

911
01:04:04.536 --> 01:04:07.876
They did what they want to because nobody knew.

912
01:04:07.876 --> 01:04:12.330
And mostly what they do, I guess they do it at night.

913
01:04:12.330 --> 01:04:14.186
They pump stuff in there.

914
01:04:14.186 --> 01:04:17.155
The whole river was contaminated at one time.

915
01:04:17.155 --> 01:04:20.866
But that's a part of the cleanup now, I guess.

916
01:04:20.866 --> 01:04:23.835
See, they got all these regulations on them.

917
01:04:23.835 --> 01:04:26.804
But see, we saw all of that change.

918
01:04:26.804 --> 01:04:32.742
But it wasn't too much of it . . . And nobody really said anything or griped about it,

919
01:04:32.742 --> 01:04:36.825
because everybody made a living out of all of the plants.

920
01:04:36.825 --> 01:04:40.165
And that was big money for people in Mossville.

921
01:04:40.165 --> 01:04:46.845
The homes and the cars and . . . These people were able like to have two cars in one family.

922
01:04:46.845 --> 01:04:50.928
Buy their kids a car, but all because of the plants.

923
01:04:50.928 --> 01:04:56.866
So I don’t have too much gripe against it myself, because I know what they done.

924
01:04:56.866 --> 01:04:58.351
It's for the job.

925
01:04:58.351 --> 01:05:03.175
Many kids' education has come because they parents was working at a plant.

926
01:05:03.175 --> 01:05:08.000
So I guess it been good and it's been bad, sort of.

927
01:05:08.000 --> 01:05:12.098
And I've always been in church, the same church.

928
01:05:12.098 --> 01:05:14.829
Well, I grew up as Methodist.

929
01:05:14.829 --> 01:05:24.846
But then all my people was Baptist, and my wife was Baptist when I met her, but see she join the Methodist.

930
01:05:24.846 --> 01:05:34.862
Then finally my uncle, he was a big deacon in the Baptist Church, Mount Zion, and he talked me into joining there.

931
01:05:34.862 --> 01:05:37.138
I been there ever since.

932
01:05:37.138 --> 01:05:40.780
And now I'm head deacon of the church.

933
01:05:40.780 --> 01:05:42.602
It's been a transition.

934
01:05:42.602 --> 01:05:48.520
Me and my wife worked in the recreation at this place right here.

935
01:05:48.520 --> 01:05:55.350
Matter of fact, all that building over there came up under me when my administration.

936
01:05:55.350 --> 01:05:57.626
The swimming pool, all that

937
01:05:57.626 --> 01:05:59.902
<c.p>came up under my administration.

938
01:05:59.902 --> 01:06:04.000
So I worked at recreation for thirty-something years.

939
01:06:04.000 --> 01:06:04.423
<v DRAGOON>Wow.

940
01:06:04.423 --> 01:06:13.722
<v WILLIAMS>But that's where I made a mistake in then paying retirement because it was considered part-time, but see I was working full-time.

941
01:06:13.722 --> 01:06:17.103
Work all the summers, work during the winter.

942
01:06:17.103 --> 01:06:22.598
That's why Butch Lemelle can tell you, that I never took a vacation.

943
01:06:22.598 --> 01:06:24.711
When school was out . . . recreation.

944
01:06:24.711 --> 01:06:27.670
I didn't know what a vacation was.

945
01:06:27.670 --> 01:06:29.361
That was my vacation.

946
01:06:29.361 --> 01:06:34.433
But as far as taking a vacation, taking off, taking a trip,

947
01:06:34.433 --> 01:06:42.464
I didn't know what that was until when I got to be a principal and we start taking vacations.

948
01:06:42.464 --> 01:06:45.000
Well, all . . . everything was work.

949
01:06:45.000 --> 01:06:48.117
<v DRAGOON>How long did you live in Mossville?

950
01:06:48.117 --> 01:06:49.453
<v WILLIAMS>All my life.

951
01:06:49.453 --> 01:06:52.124
<v DRAGOON>You moved around a little bit?

952
01:06:52.124 --> 01:06:53.905
<v WILLIAMS>Did I do what?

953
01:06:53.905 --> 01:06:56.577
<v DRAGOON>Did you move around at all?

954
01:06:56.577 --> 01:06:57.912
<v WILLIAMS>Not that much.

955
01:06:57.912 --> 01:07:02.365
I think the furthest . . . Not to stay, but to visit.

956
01:07:02.365 --> 01:07:04.146
I been all around.

957
01:07:04.146 --> 01:07:04.591
California.

958
01:07:04.591 --> 01:07:09.934
Like my mama . . . biological mama, my sister and brother, they moved when

959
01:07:09.934 --> 01:07:12.161
<c.p>I was in high school.

960
01:07:12.161 --> 01:07:16.168
They tried to get me to go with them.

961
01:07:16.168 --> 01:07:19.285
Her and her husband broke up . . . separated.

962
01:07:19.285 --> 01:07:24.182
They divided all they had, and they moved to Philadelphia, Pennsylvania.

963
01:07:24.182 --> 01:07:33.978
And that's where my auntie, my biological mama, my sister and brother . . . Matter of fact, I talked to my brother last week.

964
01:07:33.978 --> 01:07:37.095
My sister died about three years ago.

965
01:07:37.095 --> 01:07:40.212
But they all moved to Pennsylvania . . . Philadelphia.

966
01:07:40.212 --> 01:07:46.000
Well, we would go up there about every summer just to visit.

967
01:07:46.000 --> 01:07:48.581
Well, when I was coaching down here,

968
01:07:48.581 --> 01:07:55.954
and they learned that I had won them three state championships and we was in the playoff every year . . . Well,

969
01:07:55.954 --> 01:08:02.221
one of my sister's friends, she taught at a high school, Germantown High School on Haines Street.

970
01:08:02.221 --> 01:08:05.539
That was not far from where my mama lived.

971
01:08:05.539 --> 01:08:10.332
So one day she told the principal about me and he say, "Well,

972
01:08:10.332 --> 01:08:16.230
when he come back to town, bring him down here and I'm going to tour him.

973
01:08:16.230 --> 01:08:17.705
Go through the school.

974
01:08:17.705 --> 01:08:19.548
Walk him through the school."

975
01:08:19.548 --> 01:08:20.286
He did.

976
01:08:20.286 --> 01:08:24.710
Because they have school up there all year round, summer and winter.

977
01:08:24.710 --> 01:08:29.502
So I went, and he took me, walked me all through the school.

978
01:08:29.502 --> 01:08:31.714
You talk about a big school.

979
01:08:31.714 --> 01:08:33.189
And that's city school.

980
01:08:33.189 --> 01:08:39.088
He say, "If you're interested in moving up here . . .” Say, “We got a job for you."

981
01:08:39.088 --> 01:08:41.300
But this is what got me.

982
01:08:41.300 --> 01:08:43.143
I've always been a disciplinarian.

983
01:08:43.143 --> 01:08:50.885
And I walked down and I observed, and some of them classrooms, kids had their heads down, some was playing checkers.

984
01:08:50.885 --> 01:08:53.097
I say, “They wasn't paying attention.

985
01:08:53.097 --> 01:08:54.940
They put their heads down.

986
01:08:54.940 --> 01:09:00.101
They was doing. . .” I say, "Oh no, ain't no way I can take that."

987
01:09:00.101 --> 01:09:08.949
And some of them could walk off the campus and go to the little canteen and get something and come on back to school.

988
01:09:08.949 --> 01:09:11.530
I said, "No, no. Can't handle that.

989
01:09:11.530 --> 01:09:14.479
That ain't . . . I got to have some discipline."

990
01:09:14.479 --> 01:09:20.009
I know I wouldn't have made it there, and that's why I never did it.

991
01:09:20.009 --> 01:09:21.853
But we'd go up there.

992
01:09:21.853 --> 01:09:24.433
And my wife, she don't like snow.

993
01:09:24.433 --> 01:09:29.963
So we made a [convention?] one year, and we went up there during the winter.

994
01:09:29.963 --> 01:09:31.806
It didn't snow that winter.

995
01:09:31.806 --> 01:09:37.336
So two years later, my auntie had a stroke, and it was during the winter.

996
01:09:37.336 --> 01:09:41.023
So we went up there, and boy the second day

997
01:09:41.023 --> 01:09:43.235
<c.p>we was up there, it snowed.

998
01:09:43.235 --> 01:09:46.553
And see, them houses up there got three levels.

999
01:09:46.553 --> 01:09:55.401
And when she looked out there and saw that and the cars parked on the street, the snow was all the way covered them.

1000
01:09:55.401 --> 01:10:00.931
And she said, and then we had to shovel to get out of the door.

1001
01:10:00.931 --> 01:10:06.461
When she saw that, she said, "No, I ain't coming back here no more." [Laughs].

1002
01:10:06.461 --> 01:10:13.097
But see it didn’t bother me, because when I was coming up, it snowed here a couple times.

1003
01:10:13.097 --> 01:10:16.046
But since then, very seldom we get snow.

1004
01:10:16.046 --> 01:10:20.470
But they get it up there three or four times a year.

1005
01:10:20.470 --> 01:10:25.263
Well she saw that, and she’ll tell you now she ain't going back.

1006
01:10:25.263 --> 01:10:26.000
No.

1007
01:10:26.000 --> 01:10:33.074
<v DRAGOON>Can you tell me more about what this area looked like when you were coming up?

1008
01:10:33.074 --> 01:10:35.726
<v WILLIAMS>We didn't have but one street.

1009
01:10:35.726 --> 01:10:37.495
This one right here.

1010
01:10:37.495 --> 01:10:40.147
It was called Old Spanish Trail.

1011
01:10:40.147 --> 01:10:45.453
And it wasn't black top, but it was like a rock cement.

1012
01:10:45.453 --> 01:10:55.621
And when cars would pass each other, they'd have to get off on the side, because it wasn't wide enough for two cars.

1013
01:10:55.621 --> 01:10:59.158
And after they passed, you get back on.

1014
01:10:59.158 --> 01:11:01.368
That was Old Spanish Trail.

1015
01:11:01.368 --> 01:11:08.000
Then it went all the way from, I guess, from here to Orange, Texas.

1016
01:11:08.000 --> 01:11:11.600
<v DRAGOON>And that was the main road through Mossville?

1017
01:11:11.600 --> 01:11:13.850
<v WILLIAMS>That was the main road.

1018
01:11:13.850 --> 01:11:14.300
Right.

1019
01:11:14.300 --> 01:11:20.150
And they had ninety, old 90, which is part of the interstate now.

1020
01:11:20.150 --> 01:11:23.300
But that was a highway, main highway.

1021
01:11:23.300 --> 01:11:30.050
Most of that was in a white area, and it went through . . . on to Vinton.

1022
01:11:30.050 --> 01:11:36.350
And you had to cross a bridge, like a drawbridge, to go to Texas.

1023
01:11:36.350 --> 01:11:44.000
But most of this road went to the sulfur mines, all the way through from Westlake, back

1024
01:11:44.000 --> 01:11:45.350
<c.p>to Lake Charles.

1025
01:11:45.350 --> 01:11:52.100
And a lot of the loggers, they used the river to float their logs on.

1026
01:11:52.100 --> 01:11:58.400
They had a sawmill, a big sawmill in Westlake, and had one in Sulphur.

1027
01:11:58.400 --> 01:12:00.200
I saw some changes.

1028
01:12:00.200 --> 01:12:07.400
Most of the people in Mossville lived along this Old Spanish Trail, all the way down.

1029
01:12:07.400 --> 01:12:11.900
Now these subdivisions, they come up back in the eighties.

1030
01:12:11.900 --> 01:12:18.200
The Bel Air subdivision, all that was . . . Because you didn’t have that at first.

1031
01:12:18.200 --> 01:12:20.450
We didn't have no subdivision.

1032
01:12:20.450 --> 01:12:27.650
Lincoln Heights, Bel Air, and the one up here, so those come up in the eighties.

1033
01:12:27.650 --> 01:12:33.050
And these are people who moved in here from some other area.

1034
01:12:33.050 --> 01:12:38.000
<v DRAGOON>There's a lot of people moving in in the eighties?

1035
01:12:38.000 --> 01:12:46.920
<v WILLIAMS>Yeah, because one time we had nine hundred and some registered voters right here in Mossville.

1036
01:12:46.920 --> 01:12:55.841
So that was the good old political days, because then Mossville, we had some political leaders.

1037
01:12:55.841 --> 01:12:57.513
<v DRAGOON>Who were those?

1038
01:12:57.513 --> 01:13:01.416
<v WILLIAMS>Rigmaidens and . . . I think basically the Rigmaidens.

1039
01:13:01.416 --> 01:13:04.761
They called them the political leaders.

1040
01:13:04.761 --> 01:13:14.796
And what they'd do, these guys, they were running for office, they'd give them money to support them.

1041
01:13:14.796 --> 01:13:18.699
So Mossville became known as block voter.

1042
01:13:18.699 --> 01:13:20.929
See, they'd block vote.

1043
01:13:20.929 --> 01:13:25.947
That's how a lot of these politicians got in,

1044
01:13:25.947 --> 01:13:37.655
because Mossville was one of the main boxes that they'd . . . Because they'd know everybody was going to vote the same way.

1045
01:13:37.655 --> 01:13:39.327
<v DRAGOON>Do you . . . Oh.

1046
01:13:39.327 --> 01:13:41.000
<v WILLIAMS>Go ahead.

1047
01:13:41.000 --> 01:13:41.425
<v DRAGOON>Sorry.

1048
01:13:41.425 --> 01:13:44.829
Do you remember the first time you voted?

1049
01:13:44.829 --> 01:13:45.680
<v WILLIAMS>Just about.

1050
01:13:45.680 --> 01:13:52.062
Because when I did, when I was eighteen years old, I was involved in politics.

1051
01:13:52.062 --> 01:13:54.615
I even drove for the candidates.

1052
01:13:54.615 --> 01:13:57.593
See, and they'd pay you so much.

1053
01:13:57.593 --> 01:14:01.847
I wouldn't drive my car, I’d drive somebody else's car.

1054
01:14:01.847 --> 01:14:03.975
Because I didn't have one.

1055
01:14:03.975 --> 01:14:05.676
<v DRAGOON>You drove for them?

1056
01:14:05.676 --> 01:14:07.378
<v WILLIAMS>Well, yes I did.

1057
01:14:07.378 --> 01:14:12.484
Yeah, to pick up the people to bring them to the polls.

1058
01:14:12.484 --> 01:14:14.611
Yeah, I know them anytime.

1059
01:14:14.611 --> 01:14:18.015
And I think I'd get about twenty-five dollars.

1060
01:14:18.015 --> 01:14:20.567
That was a lot of money.

1061
01:14:20.567 --> 01:14:25.673
But my people always, for some reason, had me involved in politics.

1062
01:14:25.673 --> 01:14:33.331
That's where I . . . I guess that's why I'm still on the Calcasieu Parish Planning and Zoning Board now.

1063
01:14:33.331 --> 01:14:43.116
I ran for police jury one year and got in a run off, and the white guy beat me by about thirty-four votes.

1064
01:14:43.116 --> 01:14:45.244
<v DRAGOON>What were you running for?

1065
01:14:45.244 --> 01:14:46.095
<v WILLIAMS>Police jury.

1066
01:14:46.095 --> 01:14:48.647
I got beat by thirty-four votes.

1067
01:14:48.647 --> 01:14:52.476
And the run-off had started with five of us.

1068
01:14:52.476 --> 01:15:02.687
Believe it or not, where I got hurt was right where I grew up, because the white people in Westlake, they voted for me.

1069
01:15:02.687 --> 01:15:06.516
But the thirty-four votes I lost was right here.

1070
01:15:06.516 --> 01:15:11.622
That was some black people who . . . they got them everywhere, I guess.

1071
01:15:11.622 --> 01:15:20.556
They figure you're going to get too powerful and then you're going to do this and you got jealousy and envy.

1072
01:15:20.556 --> 01:15:22.258
They did me a

1073
01:15:22.258 --> 01:15:23.960
<c.p>favor, I guess [Laughs].

1074
01:15:23.960 --> 01:15:27.789
But ever since then I've been on the zone.

1075
01:15:27.789 --> 01:15:30.342
I think I'm the oldest one.

1076
01:15:30.342 --> 01:15:32.895
I’ve been there about thirty-something years.

1077
01:15:32.895 --> 01:15:34.596
Fifteen on the board.

1078
01:15:34.596 --> 01:15:38.000
<v DRAGOON>What do you do on the board?

1079
01:15:38.000 --> 01:15:40.980
<v WILLIAMS>Calcasieu parish police zoning . . . planning and zoning.

1080
01:15:40.980 --> 01:15:47.364
Like all these areas got to be zoned for anything going on on that board.

1081
01:15:47.364 --> 01:15:49.493
We deal with the plants.

1082
01:15:49.493 --> 01:15:53.749
We deal with buildings, developments, anything that's to be built,

1083
01:15:53.749 --> 01:16:02.688
they got to go to the zoning board to make sure the right specifications . . . even when they build a house now.

1084
01:16:02.688 --> 01:16:09.499
It used to be you had property, you going to build what you want on it.

1085
01:16:09.499 --> 01:16:11.627
You can't do that now.

1086
01:16:11.627 --> 01:16:19.714
You got to get permits to make sure you do it right, or you don't build in the wetlands.

1087
01:16:19.714 --> 01:16:26.099
You can build in the wetland, but you got to elevate it to a certain . . .

1088
01:16:26.099 --> 01:16:33.761
<v DRAGOON>What have you seen . . . Since you've been on the zoning board, what have you seen with this area?

1089
01:16:33.761 --> 01:16:35.889
<v WILLIAMS>I've seen all the changes.

1090
01:16:35.889 --> 01:16:39.294
See, we did all the zoning for Sasol.

1091
01:16:39.294 --> 01:16:42.700
Any of these plants . . . Like last week . . . yeah,

1092
01:16:42.700 --> 01:16:46.956
last week we had . . . last month we had Westlake Parliament.

1093
01:16:46.956 --> 01:16:50.787
I don't know you all heard of that one.

1094
01:16:50.787 --> 01:16:53.767
Now that was a real hot issue.

1095
01:16:53.767 --> 01:16:56.746
Westlake Parliament is located in Vincent Settlement.

1096
01:16:56.746 --> 01:16:58.449
That's west of here.

1097
01:16:58.449 --> 01:17:02.706
Well, this plant has been there for years, Westlake Parliament.

1098
01:17:02.706 --> 01:17:08.665
But the plant is on the east side of a main road, [Bodan?] Road.

1099
01:17:08.665 --> 01:17:15.901
It goes all the way to back to Vincent Settlement, going towards Cameron, Hackberry, all that area.

1100
01:17:15.901 --> 01:17:20.583
And you got a lot of people who live back there.

1101
01:17:20.583 --> 01:17:23.988
But the plant is on the east side.

1102
01:17:23.988 --> 01:17:25.691
But the property on

1103
01:17:25.691 --> 01:17:29.948
<c.p>the west side is all vacant, that belongs to them.

1104
01:17:29.948 --> 01:17:32.076
So what they did now,

1105
01:17:32.076 --> 01:17:41.440
they decided they going to extend that plant and they're going to put it on the other side of that [Bodan?] Road.

1106
01:17:41.440 --> 01:17:53.359
So what they asked . . . came to us to have it abandoned . . . which made good sense because they didn't want them people driving through their plant for security reasons.

1107
01:17:53.359 --> 01:17:55.913
It was up there in droves.

1108
01:17:55.913 --> 01:18:04.000
One of them guys, white guy, called us the kangaroo court, because we didn't vote in their favor.

1109
01:18:04.000 --> 01:18:08.473
So, see any time we pass it, we meet on a Tuesday.

1110
01:18:08.473 --> 01:18:12.200
At Thursday, the police jury meets in the same room.

1111
01:18:12.200 --> 01:18:18.536
And what they do, they either vote against what we did, or either they support the same.

1112
01:18:18.536 --> 01:18:20.773
But they voted the same way.

1113
01:18:20.773 --> 01:18:23.009
They very seldom go against us.

1114
01:18:23.009 --> 01:18:23.755
Upheld it.

1115
01:18:23.755 --> 01:18:29.718
But those people, when they're mad . . . They got two more routes to get out of there.

1116
01:18:29.718 --> 01:18:33.073
But this one is the shortest route, you see.

1117
01:18:33.073 --> 01:18:37.173
But still them people don't want you going through their plant.

1118
01:18:37.173 --> 01:18:40.900
It made good sense, especially in this day and time.

1119
01:18:40.900 --> 01:18:44.627
So those are the kind of things we running with.

1120
01:18:44.627 --> 01:18:46.864
Another thing is the dirt pits.

1121
01:18:46.864 --> 01:18:49.845
Nobody wants a dirt pit in their area.

1122
01:18:49.845 --> 01:18:52.082
Well, they got to have dirt.

1123
01:18:52.082 --> 01:18:56.555
For all the plants that's coming in, they got to have dirt.

1124
01:18:56.555 --> 01:19:02.145
So these people who got land, they consider it, they going to make good money.

1125
01:19:02.145 --> 01:19:06.618
And they [still?] the supply these people with the dirt they need.

1126
01:19:06.618 --> 01:19:09.227
But nobody want it close to them.

1127
01:19:09.227 --> 01:19:15.564
They'll tell you don’t put it in your backyard, but they don't . . . Somebody got to do it.

1128
01:19:15.564 --> 01:19:19.664
So we got a man camp coming up right down here.

1129
01:19:19.664 --> 01:19:24.882
They going to build a man camp right . . . about a quarter mile from me.

1130
01:19:24.882 --> 01:19:26.000
<v DRAGOON>What's that?

1131
01:19:26.000 --> 01:19:27.279
<v WILLIAMS>A man camp.

1132
01:19:27.279 --> 01:19:29.838
They call it a man camp.

1133
01:19:29.838 --> 01:19:32.396
What it is . . . they build apartments.

1134
01:19:32.396 --> 01:19:39.218
And these people who coming in here to work to build Sasol and these other plants,

1135
01:19:39.218 --> 01:19:41.777
<c.p>they come in from other places.

1136
01:19:41.777 --> 01:19:45.188
Some of them come as far as Mexico.

1137
01:19:45.188 --> 01:19:48.173
They're contractors and all that, and workers.

1138
01:19:48.173 --> 01:19:54.569
Well these are places where they provide places for them to stay while they're here.

1139
01:19:54.569 --> 01:20:00.538
So we got two of them going to come up right here in Mossville.

1140
01:20:00.538 --> 01:20:03.096
Well they're all over the Sulphur,

1141
01:20:03.096 --> 01:20:10.345
but nobody wants them because just like they say . . . What it is . . . running for president? [Donald] Trump.

1142
01:20:10.345 --> 01:20:12.051
Don't want no refugees.

1143
01:20:12.051 --> 01:20:13.756
Keep the refugees out.

1144
01:20:13.756 --> 01:20:18.447
Well, they say . . . These people say they're going to bring crime,

1145
01:20:18.447 --> 01:20:26.548
they going to bring drugs . . . Well I guess it is going . . . But the plants got to have these workers.

1146
01:20:26.548 --> 01:20:28.680
Where they going to stay?

1147
01:20:28.680 --> 01:20:35.503
They don't have enough hotels for . . . They got to build places for these people to stay.

1148
01:20:35.503 --> 01:20:38.487
So we have to deal with that.

1149
01:20:38.487 --> 01:20:46.589
<v DRAGOON>How have you . . . Has it been hard for you to be on that committee and be zoning this area?

1150
01:20:46.589 --> 01:20:50.000
Have you had conversations with people, or . . .

1151
01:20:50.000 --> 01:20:52.844
<v WILLIAMS>No. No. It's been a pleasure for me.

1152
01:20:52.844 --> 01:20:54.267
It don't bother me . . .

1153
01:20:54.267 --> 01:20:54.622
<v DRAGOON>Yeah.

1154
01:20:54.622 --> 01:20:56.756
<v WILLIAMS>. . . because I can take the heat.

1155
01:20:56.756 --> 01:20:58.889
I've been on there so long.

1156
01:20:58.889 --> 01:21:03.867
And what I do . . . I'm just trying to do the right thing, that's all.

1157
01:21:03.867 --> 01:21:07.778
But I guess this area is just like any other area.

1158
01:21:07.778 --> 01:21:10.622
It's hard for people to want to change.

1159
01:21:10.622 --> 01:21:13.822
They don't want to . . . Once you're faced with it,

1160
01:21:13.822 --> 01:21:20.222
you have people that they move out in a country area where they want to be to themselves,

1161
01:21:20.222 --> 01:21:24.133
peace and quiet, but they don't realize some changes that's coming.

1162
01:21:24.133 --> 01:21:26.978
And when them changes come, they fight it.

1163
01:21:26.978 --> 01:21:34.089
But I tell them, "If you want to move out there and you want to be alone, you better buy

1164
01:21:34.089 --> 01:21:37.289
<c.p>enough property to keep anything from being around you."

1165
01:21:37.289 --> 01:21:43.689
You can't keep people from selling their property down the street or across or in front of you.

1166
01:21:43.689 --> 01:21:45.467
That's what we're faced with.

1167
01:21:45.467 --> 01:21:47.244
But it's got to happen.

1168
01:21:47.244 --> 01:21:49.378
<v DRAGOON>Do you still live in Mossville?

1169
01:21:49.378 --> 01:21:50.444
<v WILLIAMS>Yes, still do.

1170
01:21:50.444 --> 01:21:52.222
Been here all my life.

1171
01:21:52.222 --> 01:21:54.000
<v DRAGOON>In the same house?

1172
01:21:54.000 --> 01:21:57.708
<v WILLIAMS>Well, I was raised down where the school is.

1173
01:21:57.708 --> 01:22:04.711
But after they bought us out—I think that was in the ‘50s—I still had property down there,

1174
01:22:04.711 --> 01:22:10.891
but I bought up here on Pattie Moss Road, which is not far from here.

1175
01:22:10.891 --> 01:22:14.599
<v DRAGOON>They bought . . . Who bought you out in the '50s?

1176
01:22:14.599 --> 01:22:15.423
<v WILLIAMS>The state.

1177
01:22:15.423 --> 01:22:17.070
<v DRAGOON>To build the school?

1178
01:22:17.070 --> 01:22:19.542
<v WILLIAMS>No, they build that high line.

1179
01:22:19.542 --> 01:22:22.838
You noticed down there, them big high lines?

1180
01:22:22.838 --> 01:22:25.722
All that went across the Williams' property.

1181
01:22:25.722 --> 01:22:29.430
Conoco bought the rest of it, Vista and Conoco.

1182
01:22:29.430 --> 01:22:31.901
They went in, wanted the expansion.

1183
01:22:31.901 --> 01:22:35.197
But I built the split-level house . . . five bedrooms

1184
01:22:35.197 --> 01:22:36.021
<c.p>up there.

1185
01:22:36.021 --> 01:22:38.081
And that's where I've been.

1186
01:22:38.081 --> 01:22:44.673
Now Sasol been trying to buy me out, but they ain't come up with enough money.

1187
01:22:44.673 --> 01:22:50.440
Because see my house got tore down, had to rebuild it from Hurricane Rita.

1188
01:22:50.440 --> 01:22:58.680
So my house is only about ten or eleven years old, because they had to go from the ground up.

1189
01:22:58.680 --> 01:23:04.035
And I added some things that I didn't have in the first one.

1190
01:23:04.035 --> 01:23:06.919
What they offered me is not enough.

1191
01:23:06.919 --> 01:23:09.391
They showed me a nice home.

1192
01:23:09.391 --> 01:23:13.099
Me and my wife went out there to look.

1193
01:23:13.099 --> 01:23:22.574
Five bedrooms, six acres of property, but it's out in the same area where a lot of people trying to get out of,

1194
01:23:22.574 --> 01:23:26.694
Vincent Settlement, where them plants are moving in over there.

1195
01:23:26.694 --> 01:23:34.521
It's off of 27, and 27 . . . Highway 27 runs all the way from DeQuincy all the way to Cameron.

1196
01:23:34.521 --> 01:23:36.169
That's the main highway.

1197
01:23:36.169 --> 01:23:42.349
And then they got 108 that goes all the way to Texas line to Vinton.

1198
01:23:42.349 --> 01:23:44.820
Well, that's the only two arteries.

1199
01:23:44.820 --> 01:23:48.116
So you got all kind of traffic going.

1200
01:23:48.116 --> 01:23:51.000
And it's going to get worse.

1201
01:23:51.000 --> 01:23:57.867
But the house is nice, but the house is forty years old, but it's been well-kept.

1202
01:23:57.867 --> 01:24:02.159
They got fruit trees and all that in the backyard.

1203
01:24:02.159 --> 01:24:12.459
I told them when I sat down with Sasol the last time, I said, "This is going to be six miles out of Sulphur."

1204
01:24:12.459 --> 01:24:17.180
I said, "Where I am now, I'm five minutes from Wal-Mart.

1205
01:24:17.180 --> 01:24:21.043
I'm ten minutes from either hospital, east or west."

1206
01:24:21.043 --> 01:24:28.339
I said, "I'm only ten miles from . . . ten minutes from getting my grandkids to and from school.

1207
01:24:28.339 --> 01:24:32.202
Or Market Basket and all that is around me."

1208
01:24:32.202 --> 01:24:40.785
I said, "I'm going out here six miles out of Sulphur where I got to come back in this area,

1209
01:24:40.785 --> 01:24:45.936
plus I'm going to use more gas and I'm taking a risk."

1210
01:24:45.936 --> 01:24:49.369
So you got to look at all that.

1211
01:24:49.369 --> 01:24:57.953
So I told them, I said, "The only way I can move is if y'all give me a million dollars."

1212
01:24:57.953 --> 01:25:03.961
And I knew they weren't going to do that, so that's where we are.

1213
01:25:03.961 --> 01:25:08.682
The house is nice, but the traffic thing is so bad.

1214
01:25:08.682 --> 01:25:13.833
You don't have no buses out there because it's in the country.

1215
01:25:13.833 --> 01:25:15.979
Everybody got their own automobile.

1216
01:25:15.979 --> 01:25:25.850
And I guess if you have to call an ambulance out there, it will take them twenty minutes or more to get there.

1217
01:25:25.850 --> 01:25:29.712
So a lot of people don't consider those things.

1218
01:25:29.712 --> 01:25:31.000
I do.

1219
01:25:31.000 --> 01:25:34.259
<v DRAGOON>I want to go back a little bit.

1220
01:25:34.259 --> 01:25:34.667
<v WILLIAMS>Okay.

1221
01:25:34.667 --> 01:25:43.630
<v DRAGOON>And ask you about what your life was like during the civil rights movement, and what you were doing in the '60s.

1222
01:25:43.630 --> 01:25:51.778
I know you talked about driving for campaigns, and maybe who those candidates were, and why you were supporting them.

1223
01:25:51.778 --> 01:25:53.407
<v WILLIAMS>Well, I supported them.

1224
01:25:53.407 --> 01:26:00.741
Like I said, we had political leaders, like the Rigmaidens and . . . I'm trying to think that other group.

1225
01:26:00.741 --> 01:26:01.963
But basically Rigmaiden.

1226
01:26:01.963 --> 01:26:07.667
But whatever they said was the best candidate, that's the way we basically went.

1227
01:26:07.667 --> 01:26:10.111
Now, today I don't do that.

1228
01:26:10.111 --> 01:26:12.148
I use my own judgment.

1229
01:26:12.148 --> 01:26:20.296
I don't vote on the person or party; I vote with the person I stand for, because I'm a Democrat.

1230
01:26:20.296 --> 01:26:26.000
But what I look at what the person intends to do to . . . Accountable person.

1231
01:26:26.000 --> 01:26:28.852
But back then we didn't do that.

1232
01:26:28.852 --> 01:26:35.778
Whoever they said, "This is the man we're going to support," that's who we're going to support.

1233
01:26:35.778 --> 01:26:39.444
But you wind up, I guess like it is,

1234
01:26:39.444 --> 01:26:46.778
these same people that sometimes you have to go to them for some kind of favor in return.

1235
01:26:46.778 --> 01:26:50.037
So that's all part of politics, you see?

1236
01:26:50.037 --> 01:26:59.000
But the biggest races we always had down here when I was growing up was the sheriff and the DA [District Attorney].

1237
01:26:59.000 --> 01:27:00.630
That was the biggest.

1238
01:27:00.630 --> 01:27:05.926
That's always going . . . I'd say the sheriff was bigger than the governor's race.

1239
01:27:05.926 --> 01:27:08.370
But that was the biggest thing.

1240
01:27:08.370 --> 01:27:10.000
<v DRAGOON>Why was that?

1241
01:27:10.000 --> 01:27:11.146
<v WILLIAMS>Why is it?

1242
01:27:11.146 --> 01:27:15.348
I guess because these are local people that you got to

1243
01:27:15.348 --> 01:27:18.404
<c.p>deal with on a daily basis . . . The DA.

1244
01:27:18.404 --> 01:27:24.135
The only time I had to go to the DA was to help other people.

1245
01:27:24.135 --> 01:27:29.865
Well, I had to go and help one of my boys a couple of times.

1246
01:27:29.865 --> 01:27:35.978
And the sheriff, I got to deal with him in helping and for protection and all.

1247
01:27:35.978 --> 01:27:40.180
Well, see the governor, legislators, very seldom you see these people.

1248
01:27:40.180 --> 01:27:49.348
The only time you probably see them is when they come down and want you to vote, and you don't see them no more.

1249
01:27:49.348 --> 01:27:55.461
But the sheriff and the DA, you see these people on a daily basis, just about.

1250
01:27:55.461 --> 01:28:03.483
The jury just . . . But I think the sheriff race and the DA's race is still the big thing in Calcasieu Parish.

1251
01:28:03.483 --> 01:28:05.393
I don't know about the . . .

1252
01:28:05.393 --> 01:28:09.978
<v DRAGOON>What do you remember about political activism in the '60s around here?

1253
01:28:09.978 --> 01:28:10.742
<v WILLIAMS>Like segregation?

1254
01:28:10.742 --> 01:28:11.124
<v DRAGOON>Yeah.

1255
01:28:11.124 --> 01:28:18.000
During segregation, during the civil rights movement, how that affected Mossville and your life during that time.

1256
01:28:18.000 --> 01:28:18.531
<v WILLIAMS>Yeah.

1257
01:28:18.531 --> 01:28:23.843
Well we was always involved in the civil rights movement.

1258
01:28:23.843 --> 01:28:30.217
That's how I become president of the NAACP for so many years.

1259
01:28:30.217 --> 01:28:32.872
<v DRAGOON>When did you become president?

1260
01:28:32.872 --> 01:28:34.466
<v WILLIAMS>Oh, let's see.

1261
01:28:34.466 --> 01:28:37.653
I was president for thirty years.

1262
01:28:37.653 --> 01:28:41.371
I think it was in 1970- something.

1263
01:28:41.371 --> 01:28:45.089
I was young . . . Well I say young.

1264
01:28:45.089 --> 01:28:50.932
But I became president and . . . The real serious things we had,

1265
01:28:50.932 --> 01:28:58.368
as far as the civil rights movement, was back in the 70's and 80's.

1266
01:28:58.368 --> 01:28:59.961
That's when the

1267
01:28:59.961 --> 01:29:01.555
<c.p>NAACP sued industry.

1268
01:29:01.555 --> 01:29:02.617
These plants.

1269
01:29:02.617 --> 01:29:12.178
Because you had a lot of black workers that wasn't promoted in the right positions that they deserved,

1270
01:29:12.178 --> 01:29:16.958
and then again they were having different pay scales.

1271
01:29:16.958 --> 01:29:29.175
Here were a black guy and a white guy working side by side doing the same work, but the black guy getting less.

1272
01:29:29.175 --> 01:29:35.018
So we had to fight all that, and we won it.

1273
01:29:35.018 --> 01:29:41.392
The result is you have a lot of blacks, then, became supervisors.

1274
01:29:41.392 --> 01:29:49.359
That was the biggest case, and that was a big fight back there for us.

1275
01:29:49.359 --> 01:29:54.671
And then the school situation came up too for integration,

1276
01:29:54.671 --> 01:30:03.700
because what they were doing in the black . . . in the white school when they finished their textbooks,

1277
01:30:03.700 --> 01:30:07.418
they'd pass it to the black school.

1278
01:30:07.418 --> 01:30:16.448
So we had to fight all that to make sure we got equal rights and equipment . . . textbooks.

1279
01:30:16.448 --> 01:30:22.291
They done already use the textbooks, and they'd get new ones.

1280
01:30:22.291 --> 01:30:27.602
So all those things became a reality in this movement.

1281
01:30:27.602 --> 01:30:30.789
Another thing is involved in sit-ins.

1282
01:30:30.789 --> 01:30:40.881
That is in the downtown areas, and also in Mossville, because they had separate water fountains, separate restroom facilities,

1283
01:30:40.881 --> 01:30:49.380
separate . . . even in the bus station they had separate sitting areas, so we fought all that.

1284
01:30:49.380 --> 01:30:58.941
But we started first with JC Penney's in the restaurant area, because blacks couldn't go to the counter.

1285
01:30:58.941 --> 01:31:11.157
You could go in there and get something, but you had to go to the end of the line and then go outside.

1286
01:31:11.157 --> 01:31:14.344
So we had sit-ins on that.

1287
01:31:14.344 --> 01:31:17.000
<v DRAGOON>What year was that?

1288
01:31:17.000 --> 01:31:19.394
<v WILLIAMS>That was in the '80s . . .

1289
01:31:19.394 --> 01:31:19.873
<v DRAGOON>Really?

1290
01:31:19.873 --> 01:31:21.789
<v WILLIAMS>. . . part of the '80s.

1291
01:31:21.789 --> 01:31:24.662
So all that we saw change.

1292
01:31:24.662 --> 01:31:31.845
But you'd be in . . . When I was growing up, on Ryan Street in Lake Charles,

1293
01:31:31.845 --> 01:31:43.338
if you walking on the sidewalk and a white person was coming towards you, you had to get off, and then get back on.

1294
01:31:43.338 --> 01:31:45.732
It was just that bad.

1295
01:31:45.732 --> 01:31:48.606
And it was bad in Westlake.

1296
01:31:48.606 --> 01:31:49.563
<v DRAGOON>How so?

1297
01:31:49.563 --> 01:31:52.915
<v WILLIAMS>When the schools got . . . the schools integrated.

1298
01:31:52.915 --> 01:32:03.451
When they closed Mossville, all the black kids had to go to Westlake High School because the kids in Sulphur and Vinton,

1299
01:32:03.451 --> 01:32:10.155
they all had to come to Mossville for . . . they didn't have a high school.

1300
01:32:10.155 --> 01:32:21.169
So when they integrated, the majority of the kids had to go to Westlake, and some had to go to Sulphur High School.

1301
01:32:21.169 --> 01:32:24.521
So the white people didn't want to.

1302
01:32:24.521 --> 01:32:31.225
So on the very first week when the buses drove up to the school,

1303
01:32:31.225 --> 01:32:37.930
they had ropes tied up in the trees, like they were going to hang.

1304
01:32:37.930 --> 01:32:41.761
And that's what started all the racial fights.

1305
01:32:41.761 --> 01:32:47.507
Now I'm working down there now as a coach when this happened.

1306
01:32:47.507 --> 01:32:52.775
So they had so many racial fights, you wouldn't believe it.

1307
01:32:52.775 --> 01:32:54.690
<v DRAGOON>What kind of fights?

1308
01:32:54.690 --> 01:32:56.606
What was going on?

1309
01:32:56.606 --> 01:32:59.000
What were you seeing?

1310
01:32:59.000 --> 01:33:02.184
<v WILLIAMS>They didn't want the blacks in their school.

1311
01:33:02.184 --> 01:33:04.969
And the blacks didn't want to go.

1312
01:33:04.969 --> 01:33:07.357
But they had closed their school.

1313
01:33:07.357 --> 01:33:09.745
That's where they had to go.

1314
01:33:09.745 --> 01:33:11.735
The whites didn't want them.

1315
01:33:11.735 --> 01:33:15.714
And that's where the fights . . . Now, I've seen this happen.

1316
01:33:15.714 --> 01:33:19.694
They had . . . This is going to sound funny to you.

1317
01:33:19.694 --> 01:33:23.276
They had restrooms, they closed then just like this.

1318
01:33:23.276 --> 01:33:24.071
No windows.

1319
01:33:24.071 --> 01:33:31.633
So in the break time, when the boys . . . most of the boys . . . it wasn't too much of the girls.

1320
01:33:31.633 --> 01:33:32.031
But

1321
01:33:32.031 --> 01:33:34.418
<c.p>the boys, they'd go in there.

1322
01:33:34.418 --> 01:33:39.592
Well you had the white boys and the black boys in there together.

1323
01:33:39.592 --> 01:33:47.949
So what the black boys went to doing, went to cutting off the lights and beating up on the white boys.

1324
01:33:47.949 --> 01:33:53.918
They didn't know who hit him because there was dark and there ain’t no light.

1325
01:33:53.918 --> 01:33:58.296
When they cut the light on . . . Because there ain’t no windows.

1326
01:33:58.296 --> 01:34:05.459
So what they had to do, they had to fix the lights so they couldn't be cut off.

1327
01:34:05.459 --> 01:34:14.214
And then they went to station there some coaches at the recess time when . . . the coaches was located somewhere in that area.

1328
01:34:14.214 --> 01:34:17.000
But they had so many fights.

1329
01:34:17.000 --> 01:34:23.885
I seen one time when they said that they were going to do something to Mossville kids,

1330
01:34:23.885 --> 01:34:31.176
all those and Mossville . . . Butch can tell you and some of the rest of them can tell you.

1331
01:34:31.176 --> 01:34:41.301
They had about twenty of these young men and they all armed themselves and they drove all the way around that school all that day,

1332
01:34:41.301 --> 01:34:44.136
and they parked all along the back.

1333
01:34:44.136 --> 01:34:51.022
Watch and say, "Any time any white kid do," say, "We going to be on that campus."

1334
01:34:51.022 --> 01:34:53.857
And the police didn't do them nothing.

1335
01:34:53.857 --> 01:34:55.072
But nothing happened.

1336
01:34:55.072 --> 01:35:03.982
But they're all lined up and all of them had cars, and they just drove . . . paraded around that school that whole day.

1337
01:35:03.982 --> 01:35:07.627
So we had some, but it's much better now.

1338
01:35:07.627 --> 01:35:10.462
I say way better, ninety percent better.

1339
01:35:10.462 --> 01:35:15.323
But they just didn't want to accept no blacks in their school.

1340
01:35:15.323 --> 01:35:25.853
I blame the government, because the way the transition was made, it should have been made, instead of just boom, throwing it on them like that.

1341
01:35:25.853 --> 01:35:26.663
You see?

1342
01:35:26.663 --> 01:35:29.093
Nobody was really prepared for it.

1343
01:35:29.093 --> 01:35:34.358
They could see the whites didn't want it, the blacks didn't want it.

1344
01:35:34.358 --> 01:35:36.789
They didn't want their school closed.

1345
01:35:36.789 --> 01:35:47.319
And there were some other things, because Westlake wasn't winning nothing in football, and we was winning every year and we were playing in their stadium.

1346
01:35:47.319 --> 01:35:56.229
So they got together and said, "We need some of these black players, and that's the way we're going to get them."

1347
01:35:56.229 --> 01:35:58.659
And that's the way it happened.

1348
01:35:58.659 --> 01:36:01.495
There's a lot of reality in it.

1349
01:36:01.495 --> 01:36:09.190
<v DRAGOON>So folks from Mossville, when the threats were being made, they're the ones who drove and were circling that?

1350
01:36:09.190 --> 01:36:10.000
<v WILLIAMS>Yeah.

1351
01:36:10.000 --> 01:36:11.277
<v DRAGOON>Who were they?

1352
01:36:11.277 --> 01:36:12.980
Were they a group?

1353
01:36:12.980 --> 01:36:15.108
<v WILLIAMS>They were former Mossville students.

1354
01:36:15.108 --> 01:36:17.662
Some of them were football players.

1355
01:36:17.662 --> 01:36:21.493
Some of them . . . A lot of them are dead.

1356
01:36:21.493 --> 01:36:24.047
Some of them are still living.

1357
01:36:24.047 --> 01:36:30.007
But they were former students who had either dropped out or graduated from Mossville.

1358
01:36:30.007 --> 01:36:33.412
They weren't no students who were in school.

1359
01:36:33.412 --> 01:36:36.818
These were . . . Some of them were grown men,

1360
01:36:36.818 --> 01:36:44.054
and they either had their relatives going to school, or their cousins or their friends or something.

1361
01:36:44.054 --> 01:36:46.608
It could have been a war.

1362
01:36:46.608 --> 01:36:50.014
But eventually it . . . But what really triggered it,

1363
01:36:50.014 --> 01:36:58.527
when they got out that day and they saw them people up there with them ropes, that's when it started.

1364
01:36:58.527 --> 01:37:04.912
So the black kids said they weren't going to take that, and they had fights.

1365
01:37:04.912 --> 01:37:13.000
They must have had three or four fights going on at one time, girls and boys, different areas.

1366
01:37:13.000 --> 01:37:20.747
And then you had some teachers, some white teachers, that was against it and made it known to the students.

1367
01:37:20.747 --> 01:37:21.909
That was bad.

1368
01:37:21.909 --> 01:37:25.008
So they had one particularly I can remember.

1369
01:37:25.008 --> 01:37:26.945
The principal was so weak.

1370
01:37:26.945 --> 01:37:30.818
They got rid of him because he couldn't control it.

1371
01:37:30.818 --> 01:37:37.016
So they brought a professor from McNeese over there to run . . . intern to run the school.

1372
01:37:37.016 --> 01:37:43.213
So we was in a faculty meeting and this white teacher, Miss . . . what's that lady's name?

1373
01:37:43.213 --> 01:37:43.601
Kennedy.

1374
01:37:43.601 --> 01:37:53.285
She made the remark about the blacks being undesirable and he told her right there in the . . . in front of everybody on staff and said,

1375
01:37:53.285 --> 01:37:58.320
"If that's your feeling that these black students are 'undesirable,'" he said, "then

1376
01:37:58.320 --> 01:38:01.419
<c.p>you don't need to be in this school."

1377
01:38:01.419 --> 01:38:06.067
He straightened that school out, too, and it's been straight ever since.

1378
01:38:06.067 --> 01:38:09.166
But during . . . The old . . . The principal at first,

1379
01:38:09.166 --> 01:38:18.075
he thought he couldn't handle it because he never dealt with that kind of problems, and never dealt with black kids and white.

1380
01:38:18.075 --> 01:38:20.399
And he just couldn't handle it.

1381
01:38:20.399 --> 01:38:25.047
He grew up in that area, in a part of that community,

1382
01:38:25.047 --> 01:38:29.696
so he had to side with his . . . You know how that goes.

1383
01:38:29.696 --> 01:38:34.344
He just couldn’t put his foot down and say what was right.

1384
01:38:34.344 --> 01:38:36.668
He had to side with them.

1385
01:38:36.668 --> 01:38:41.316
And the more he sided with them, the worse it would get.

1386
01:38:41.316 --> 01:38:51.000
When you got leaders in there that's going to do what was right, you saw things begin to change and then make a difference.

1387
01:38:51.000 --> 01:38:55.431
<v DRAGOON>How did you deal with it as a teacher and a coach?

1388
01:38:55.431 --> 01:38:58.385
<v WILLIAMS>You know what I think about that day?

1389
01:38:58.385 --> 01:39:04.662
It really didn't bother me, because we had a problem and we had to deal with it.

1390
01:39:04.662 --> 01:39:08.723
So, I had to kind of control it on both sides.

1391
01:39:08.723 --> 01:39:12.785
I couldn't show a difference, and I didn't show a difference.

1392
01:39:12.785 --> 01:39:20.169
I didn't show a difference with the white kids, I didn't show a difference with . . . And they got some white,

1393
01:39:20.169 --> 01:39:27.185
grown people right now that's in Westlake, and I can give you their names and you can call them.

1394
01:39:27.185 --> 01:39:30.508
I used to have a paddle all the time.

1395
01:39:30.508 --> 01:39:32.723
That's what I kept at Mossville.

1396
01:39:32.723 --> 01:39:34.938
The principal didn't deal with them.

1397
01:39:34.938 --> 01:39:39.738
When he had a problem, he'd send them to Coach in the gym.

1398
01:39:39.738 --> 01:39:42.692
And I put that paddle on their butts.

1399
01:39:42.692 --> 01:39:46.754
Well, when the schools integrated, they had some of them say,

1400
01:39:46.754 --> 01:39:52.662
"Well he going down there, them white kids now, he ain't going to use that paddle."

1401
01:39:52.662 --> 01:39:54.508
I used that same paddle.

1402
01:39:54.508 --> 01:39:57.831
They got some of them can tell you today.

1403
01:39:57.831 --> 01:39:59.677
I can tell you one.

1404
01:39:59.677 --> 01:40:06.323
If you know the police juror, Hal McMillin, ask him how many times I put the paddle on ….

1405
01:40:06.323 --> 01:40:11.123
And them boys, them grown men, come to me today and thank me.

1406
01:40:11.123 --> 01:40:13.338
So I didn't make a difference.

1407
01:40:13.338 --> 01:40:17.400
And when they saw that, it made me look bigger, really.

1408
01:40:17.400 --> 01:40:17.600
I didn't . .

1409
01:40:17.400 --> 01:40:18.138
<c.p>.

1410
01:40:18.138 --> 01:40:19.985
I wasn't looking for that.

1411
01:40:19.985 --> 01:40:27.369
But it show I didn't make a difference, because I . . . The only thing I'm concerned with is you do right,

1412
01:40:27.369 --> 01:40:31.062
and you get an education, do the best you can.

1413
01:40:31.062 --> 01:40:35.862
That's where I've always been, and that's what they give me credit for.

1414
01:40:35.862 --> 01:40:41.769
But Hal McMillin, which is now the police jury for this area, he can tell you.

1415
01:40:41.769 --> 01:40:47.677
It proved they got a lot of them, grown men, that are raising their kids today,

1416
01:40:47.677 --> 01:40:53.585
and they say, "Coach Williams put that paddle on my butt when I was growing up."

1417
01:40:53.585 --> 01:40:55.062
And they good businessmen.

1418
01:40:55.062 --> 01:40:59.862
All of them are business men raising their family, and got good jobs.

1419
01:40:59.862 --> 01:41:03.185
So it's just the way you look at it.

1420
01:41:03.185 --> 01:41:10.200
You don't . . . I never did make any separate . . . I never was taught that, either, when I was growing up.

1421
01:41:10.200 --> 01:41:15.369
When I grew up here people never taught me anything about being against whites.

1422
01:41:15.369 --> 01:41:20.538
They always taught me to respect all people, regardless of what color they were.

1423
01:41:20.538 --> 01:41:22.754
That's the way I was raised.

1424
01:41:22.754 --> 01:41:24.600
But that was some changes.

1425
01:41:24.600 --> 01:41:29.031
They had buses—another thing that we had to deal with in integration.

1426
01:41:29.031 --> 01:41:34.200
When you'd get on the bus, the blacks had to go to the back.

1427
01:41:34.200 --> 01:41:39.369
And all of the seats in the front, they had a sign up "Colored."

1428
01:41:39.369 --> 01:41:44.169
We had all that change, but the changes you had to violate it.

1429
01:41:44.169 --> 01:41:47.862
You had to go sit in that area in protest.

1430
01:41:47.862 --> 01:41:51.185
And they called the police a lot of times.

1431
01:41:51.185 --> 01:41:55.246
That didn't do any good, because they knew we was right.

1432
01:41:55.246 --> 01:42:00.415
So eventually, they took the signs down and everybody could sit anywhere you want.

1433
01:42:00.415 --> 01:42:03.000
<v DRAGOON>You were involved with those protests?

1434
01:42:03.000 --> 01:42:03.891
<v WILLIAMS>Oh, yeah.

1435
01:42:03.891 --> 01:42:05.227
All of them.

1436
01:42:05.227 --> 01:42:06.564
All of them.

1437
01:42:06.564 --> 01:42:10.128
<v DRAGOON>What kind of other protesting did you do?

1438
01:42:10.128 --> 01:42:16.365
<v WILLIAMS>That's about the only . . . Basically, we never did really get into the job factor.

1439
01:42:16.365 --> 01:42:20.820
This came in later years, because of getting equal pay.

1440
01:42:20.820 --> 01:42:23.938
Well, we fought that in the plants.

1441
01:42:23.938 --> 01:42:29.730
But there are some other areas that needed to make some adjustments also.

1442
01:42:29.730 --> 01:42:31.512
But that eventually came.

1443
01:42:31.512 --> 01:42:35.076
But I think that was the biggest thing.

1444
01:42:35.076 --> 01:42:38.640
We shut a store down out here once.

1445
01:42:38.640 --> 01:42:43.986
It was . . . The Daigles had a store on Prater Road right here.

1446
01:42:43.986 --> 01:42:49.777
Well this was a grocery store, and it was whites that owned it.

1447
01:42:49.777 --> 01:42:55.569
They had a black guy that would work in there in the store.

1448
01:42:55.569 --> 01:42:57.796
I guess he's the manager.

1449
01:42:57.796 --> 01:43:03.142
But we was told later that they were going with each other.

1450
01:43:03.142 --> 01:43:13.834
Well, people would go in the store . . . I went there one Sunday and I went to pick up some . . . get some meat or something.

1451
01:43:13.834 --> 01:43:17.398
I forgot . . . it was pork chops or something.

1452
01:43:17.398 --> 01:43:22.744
But, you know how meat is tainted . . . you can look at it?

1453
01:43:22.744 --> 01:43:24.526
And I told him.

1454
01:43:24.526 --> 01:43:28.981
He said, "Well you either take it or leave it."

1455
01:43:28.981 --> 01:43:31.654
Well, I just left the store.

1456
01:43:31.654 --> 01:43:34.773
I went back and told my wife.

1457
01:43:34.773 --> 01:43:37.000
But it irritated me.

1458
01:43:37.000 --> 01:43:45.360
About three or four weeks later, one of my football players had went in there and he had it with him.

1459
01:43:45.360 --> 01:43:47.748
I was working at the recreation.

1460
01:43:47.748 --> 01:43:53.321
He come down here and he said, "Coach, we're going to close it down."

1461
01:43:53.321 --> 01:43:56.904
And when he told me, it made me mad.

1462
01:43:56.904 --> 01:44:00.089
So what we did, we picketed that store.

1463
01:44:00.089 --> 01:44:05.264
Across the street, that house that's still there, was a black minister, Gassaway.

1464
01:44:05.264 --> 01:44:09.245
He let us put up a tent in his yard.

1465
01:44:09.245 --> 01:44:13.225
And that's where we stayed and we picketed that store.

1466
01:44:13.225 --> 01:44:14.818
They called the police.

1467
01:44:14.818 --> 01:44:19.993
Some of . . . They brought the paddy wagon out here and arrested a bunch.

1468
01:44:19.993 --> 01:44:25.168
I didn’t get arrested in it, because I probably was the last one.

1469
01:44:25.168 --> 01:44:32.731
I was heading that thing, so they put as many as they could in it and took to jail.

1470
01:44:32.731 --> 01:44:37.906
But I never forget that big old state trooper was walking behind me.

1471
01:44:37.906 --> 01:44:42.285
And I never looked back like I didn't even seen him.

1472
01:44:42.285 --> 01:44:48.655
We hid our signs and brought them back, and all he did was walk behind me.

1473
01:44:48.655 --> 01:44:53.432
Gave me a bad eye, but I didn't pay him no attention.

1474
01:44:53.432 --> 01:44:56.218
And finally we closed that store down.

1475
01:44:56.218 --> 01:45:02.189
But it wasn't because of . . . It was because of the way it was treating people.

1476
01:45:02.189 --> 01:45:04.180
That was the main reason.

1477
01:45:04.180 --> 01:45:06.966
But that was . . . We stayed there all

1478
01:45:06.966 --> 01:45:07.365
<c.p>night.

1479
01:45:07.365 --> 01:45:09.355
Every day we changed pickets.

1480
01:45:09.355 --> 01:45:13.734
Somebody else walked for an hour and another group come on.

1481
01:45:13.734 --> 01:45:15.724
But we got some attention.

1482
01:45:15.724 --> 01:45:22.890
And not only did we get attention in Mossville, this kind of spread it all over the area.

1483
01:45:22.890 --> 01:45:25.278
They saw what was going on.

1484
01:45:25.278 --> 01:45:27.269
That's some of the changes.

1485
01:45:27.269 --> 01:45:32.046
I remember only three churches they had when I was growing up.

1486
01:45:32.046 --> 01:45:36.823
They had a Baptist church, a Methodist church, and a Sanctified church.

1487
01:45:36.823 --> 01:45:39.211
Now you see several of them.

1488
01:45:39.211 --> 01:45:41.600
Some of them done sold out.

1489
01:45:41.600 --> 01:45:43.988
But that was the only three.

1490
01:45:43.988 --> 01:45:47.571
Those three churches, we all cooperated with each other.

1491
01:45:47.571 --> 01:45:52.746
If they had something at their church, we all went and pitched in.

1492
01:45:52.746 --> 01:45:57.523
Whatever activities they had . . . If they had the anniversary of every church.

1493
01:45:57.523 --> 01:45:59.513
Now it's not that way.

1494
01:45:59.513 --> 01:46:01.504
Everybody's just about separated now.

1495
01:46:01.504 --> 01:46:09.067
Everybody goes to their own church and very seldom . . . Which I think is bad for a community this small.

1496
01:46:09.067 --> 01:46:13.446
You got two or three different non-denominations they don't believe in.

1497
01:46:13.446 --> 01:46:17.029
But I don't have anything to do with it.

1498
01:46:17.029 --> 01:46:20.612
I have enough to worry about with my own.

1499
01:46:20.612 --> 01:46:23.000
There ain't but one god.

1500
01:46:23.000 --> 01:46:27.215
<v DRAGOON>So when was the street named after you?

1501
01:46:27.215 --> 01:46:29.323
<v WILLIAMS>It was in 2000-something.

1502
01:46:29.323 --> 01:46:34.591
I think it was in 2002, around 2002, I believe.

1503
01:46:34.591 --> 01:46:37.226
<v DRAGOON>How did that come about?

1504
01:46:37.226 --> 01:46:44.075
<v WILLIAMS>Well, through my community service and things I'd been doing in the community.

1505
01:46:44.075 --> 01:46:49.344
I'd been involved in everything, and do all my education.

1506
01:46:49.344 --> 01:46:55.667
The one that really caused . . . Like the Hal, I taught him school,

1507
01:46:55.667 --> 01:47:04.624
in high school, and he knew all about the things that I stood for, based on that.

1508
01:47:04.624 --> 01:47:10.946
And when he became police jury, then that's what he recommended that

1509
01:47:10.946 --> 01:47:12.000
<c.p>they do.

1510
01:47:12.000 --> 01:47:20.957
Because most times, when you get something named, they wait until you die, not while you're living.

1511
01:47:20.957 --> 01:47:29.914
And then most of the other police jurors beside him, I knew them and they knew me.

1512
01:47:29.914 --> 01:47:35.183
And like I said, they knew what I stood for.

1513
01:47:35.183 --> 01:47:38.871
I think that was the main reason.

1514
01:47:38.871 --> 01:47:47.828
I was always involved in community work all over the area, whatever I could do to help.

1515
01:47:47.828 --> 01:47:53.624
<v DRAGOON>Were you involved with the Boy Scouts at all around here?

1516
01:47:53.624 --> 01:47:55.204
<v WILLIAMS>At one time.

1517
01:47:55.204 --> 01:48:04.161
I was a Boy Scout myself, and then I was a scout master for about two years.

1518
01:48:04.161 --> 01:48:11.011
I think the reason for that is when I got these different jobs,

1519
01:48:11.011 --> 01:48:18.387
and somebody else came in and did a better job than I was doing.

1520
01:48:18.387 --> 01:48:23.656
But I’ve always been involved in something in the community.

1521
01:48:23.656 --> 01:48:24.183
Whatever.

1522
01:48:24.183 --> 01:48:31.559
A lot of times I didn't involve myself as people who involve me any.

1523
01:48:31.559 --> 01:48:36.301
I think it was good because it helped me.

1524
01:48:36.301 --> 01:48:38.935
I don't know what else.

1525
01:48:38.935 --> 01:48:50.000
<v DRAGOON>[Laughs] What do you think inspired you to become a teacher, and become a coach, and become a political leader?

1526
01:48:50.000 --> 01:48:51.568
<v WILLIAMS>That's a good question.

1527
01:48:51.568 --> 01:48:55.879
My teachers inspired me, especially . . . Well, all my teachers inspired me.

1528
01:48:55.879 --> 01:48:59.407
But the greatest inspiration came from my football coach.

1529
01:48:59.407 --> 01:49:00.974
<v DRAGOON>What was his name?

1530
01:49:00.974 --> 01:49:03.718
<v WILLIAMS>[Earl DePeece?] He was from New Orleans.

1531
01:49:03.718 --> 01:49:08.029
That was about . . . I said [?] I want to be a coach.

1532
01:49:08.029 --> 01:49:14.300
Because my mama, when I was growing up, said that she wanted me to be a

1533
01:49:14.300 --> 01:49:14.692
<c.p>preacher.

1534
01:49:14.692 --> 01:49:17.436
And I always thought of something else.

1535
01:49:17.436 --> 01:49:22.923
I said I'd like to be a lawyer, I'd like to be a doctor.

1536
01:49:22.923 --> 01:49:26.059
But that's what I had in my mind,

1537
01:49:26.059 --> 01:49:33.897
but still nobody could . . . around me could inspire me because of where I grew up in the country, you see.

1538
01:49:33.897 --> 01:49:41.736
But when I went to the city and started playing football, I said, "This is what I want to be.

1539
01:49:41.736 --> 01:49:44.088
I want to be a coach."

1540
01:49:44.088 --> 01:49:50.751
And I guess that was based on when I found out that I had some athletic ability.

1541
01:49:50.751 --> 01:49:52.711
I picked the right thing.

1542
01:49:52.711 --> 01:49:58.198
But my next thing was the military, and I often think now . . . I said,

1543
01:49:58.198 --> 01:50:04.469
well, I've been in the military, I might have been all shot up now, or dead.

1544
01:50:04.469 --> 01:50:08.388
Because you don't want to come with all the things.

1545
01:50:08.388 --> 01:50:15.051
So I picked coaching, and if I had to do it all over, I'd do it again,

1546
01:50:15.051 --> 01:50:22.498
because it put me in a position that I could be able to help young men and counsel them.

1547
01:50:22.498 --> 01:50:32.297
I saw a lot of them come from nothing to something, and they give me credit for that today, but I wasn't looking for that.

1548
01:50:32.297 --> 01:50:37.000
I was just trying to make a man out of them.

1549
01:50:37.000 --> 01:50:43.555
But in my family, my uncle was my greatest inspiration because he always had something.

1550
01:50:43.555 --> 01:50:48.799
He worked in the plant, and he always kept a new car.

1551
01:50:48.799 --> 01:50:52.732
And a lot of times he stayed with us.

1552
01:50:52.732 --> 01:50:57.976
He helped build my house . . . not my house, but my mama's house.

1553
01:50:57.976 --> 01:50:59.724
He was an electrician.

1554
01:50:59.724 --> 01:51:02.346
Well, he was a paper hanger.

1555
01:51:02.346 --> 01:51:05.406
You name it, he could do it.

1556
01:51:05.406 --> 01:51:08.465
He worked that old and then retired.

1557
01:51:08.465 --> 01:51:11.961
He's always . . . He'd take me around with him.

1558
01:51:11.961 --> 01:51:18.079
I learned how to hang paper, I learned . . . One thing I watched him do,

1559
01:51:18.079 --> 01:51:25.071
and I got burned by, I used to see him check his sparkplugs in his car.

1560
01:51:25.071 --> 01:51:27.256
I watched everything he did.

1561
01:51:27.256 --> 01:51:30.315
And he was a good baseball player.

1562
01:51:30.315 --> 01:51:33.374
He taught me how to play baseball.

1563
01:51:33.374 --> 01:51:35.122
I played semi-pro baseball.

1564
01:51:35.122 --> 01:51:41.677
We had two fields right here in Mossville, and that was the recreation on Sundays.

1565
01:51:41.677 --> 01:51:44.299
Some . . . We'd play all over Louisiana.

1566
01:51:44.299 --> 01:51:49.106
Sometimes in Texas when the teams would come, we'd go there.

1567
01:51:49.106 --> 01:51:52.165
He taught me how to plays baseball.

1568
01:51:52.165 --> 01:51:57.846
I saw him when he used to check his sparkplugs with that screwdriver.

1569
01:51:57.846 --> 01:52:06.587
So when I got a car, I bought a car of my own, and I wanted to check my sparkplugs.

1570
01:52:06.587 --> 01:52:09.646
I took that screwdriver, and when I

1571
01:52:09.646 --> 01:52:13.579
<c.p>touched that plug, it almost knocked my arm off.

1572
01:52:13.579 --> 01:52:20.134
That's when I learned then that that was a rubber handled screwdriver he was using.

1573
01:52:20.134 --> 01:52:20.571
See?

1574
01:52:20.571 --> 01:52:25.815
But I learned. [Laughs] Yeah, it liked to knock my arm off.

1575
01:52:25.815 --> 01:52:27.126
<v DRAGOON>Oh my goodness.

1576
01:52:27.126 --> 01:52:28.000
<v WILLIAMS>Yeah.

1577
01:52:28.000 --> 01:52:35.460
<v DRAGOON>I have only . . . I have a few . . . just maybe a couple more questions.

1578
01:52:35.460 --> 01:52:40.625
You've lived in Mossville for such a long time.

1579
01:52:40.625 --> 01:52:49.807
What do you want people to remember about Mossville that maybe younger folks don't know already?

1580
01:52:49.807 --> 01:53:00.136
<v WILLIAMS>I'd like for them to remember the school, the churches, and some of the families . . . the old families,

1581
01:53:00.136 --> 01:53:08.170
because those were the three things that helped us to be what we are.

1582
01:53:08.170 --> 01:53:14.483
Everything was geared around the church, the school, and the family.

1583
01:53:14.483 --> 01:53:19.074
And you had . . . I often tell them this.

1584
01:53:19.074 --> 01:53:26.534
Mossville, everybody had either a garden or they raised cattle, they raised hogs.

1585
01:53:26.534 --> 01:53:34.568
When one family killed a hog, all the other families went over and helped.

1586
01:53:34.568 --> 01:53:38.011
When they got through, they shared.

1587
01:53:38.011 --> 01:53:41.455
The same thing with the gardening.

1588
01:53:41.455 --> 01:53:44.324
They shared what they had.

1589
01:53:44.324 --> 01:53:47.193
One swapped something to another.

1590
01:53:47.193 --> 01:53:55.227
Where this one over here was raising potatoes, and this one was raising corn.

1591
01:53:55.227 --> 01:54:00.966
So everybody always had something because they shared, they swapped.

1592
01:54:00.966 --> 01:54:04.983
And that's just about my . . . Everything else.

1593
01:54:04.983 --> 01:54:09.000
Everybody tried to help one another.

1594
01:54:09.000 --> 01:54:13.844
I remember if somebody was a sick, an older person was sick,

1595
01:54:13.844 --> 01:54:23.130
the families would go over and bring us with him and they'd build a log fire, a big wood fire, like a bonfire.

1596
01:54:23.130 --> 01:54:27.974
<c.p>We'd sit up there all night with them people who were sick.

1597
01:54:27.974 --> 01:54:30.800
Wakes were not done in the church.

1598
01:54:30.800 --> 01:54:32.819
Wakes were done at home.

1599
01:54:32.819 --> 01:54:36.452
Somebody died, that wake was held in the house.

1600
01:54:36.452 --> 01:54:42.507
He would sit up all night long with that body, and drink coffee and eat.

1601
01:54:42.507 --> 01:54:44.122
So, things like that.

1602
01:54:44.122 --> 01:54:48.967
And you tell the kids like that today, they don't believe it.

1603
01:54:48.967 --> 01:54:56.233
Well you tell a lot of youngsters what we went through as far as segregation and all that,

1604
01:54:56.233 --> 01:55:03.500
they don't believe that, because a lot of them think that's been . . . it's been like this all along.

1605
01:55:03.500 --> 01:55:09.152
So they need to remember where a lot of the . . . where they came from,

1606
01:55:09.152 --> 01:55:18.437
because a lot of things that they enjoy now was built on because what people did ahead of them, and struggle and change.

1607
01:55:18.437 --> 01:55:23.685
So that's the thing that [drives?] me most with the young people today,

1608
01:55:23.685 --> 01:55:29.337
that they don't realize what has been done to get them where they are.

1609
01:55:29.337 --> 01:55:31.356
They don't have no respect.

1610
01:55:31.356 --> 01:55:35.796
They don't have no . . . I even notice that with my grandkids.

1611
01:55:35.796 --> 01:55:37.815
They take everything for granted.

1612
01:55:37.815 --> 01:55:38.622
You see?

1613
01:55:38.622 --> 01:55:43.063
They don't know how to really take responsibility for their actions.

1614
01:55:43.063 --> 01:55:47.100
Back then, see, we knew what was going to happen.

1615
01:55:47.100 --> 01:55:51.541
If you do the wrong thing, you knew what was coming.

1616
01:55:51.541 --> 01:55:53.963
But now, it's a different generation.

1617
01:55:53.963 --> 01:55:58.000
But it's hard to get it through to them.

1618
01:55:58.000 --> 01:56:03.280
I know I have trouble with my . . . the daughter that I adopted.

1619
01:56:03.280 --> 01:56:05.480
Now she's thirty years old.

1620
01:56:05.480 --> 01:56:08.120
She don't know what budget means.

1621
01:56:08.120 --> 01:56:12.520
Her and her husband, they don't know what budget means.

1622
01:56:12.520 --> 01:56:18.240
You put money in their hands, they don't think about what goes first.

1623
01:56:18.240 --> 01:56:21.760
But we was taught all that back then.

1624
01:56:21.760 --> 01:56:25.280
If you don't have it, don't buy it.

1625
01:56:25.280 --> 01:56:32.320
I had a . . . My wife's grandpa is dead, and he almost died almost a wealthy man.

1626
01:56:32.320 --> 01:56:34.960
He had his own strawberry farm.

1627
01:56:34.960 --> 01:56:41.560
He raised all kinds of vegetables, and he sold them to stores like George [Theriot?].

1628
01:56:41.560 --> 01:56:43.760
You don't . . . Y'all too young.

1629
01:56:43.760 --> 01:56:47.280
But these were stores like Market Basket now.

1630
01:56:47.280 --> 01:56:51.680
George [Theriot?] had stores all over Westlake, Lake Charles, Sulphur.

1631
01:56:51.680 --> 01:56:56.520
My wife's grandpa used to raise strawberries and all of it,

1632
01:56:56.520 --> 01:57:04.000
and he'd take it to these stores and they'd sell it to them, and they sold it.

1633
01:57:04.000 --> 01:57:10.160
<c.p>All these kinds of things happened back then, but if you tell kids this,

1634
01:57:10.160 --> 01:57:15.880
they don't even want to do that kind of work; that's too hard.

1635
01:57:15.880 --> 01:57:23.800
They don't know nothing about . . . And I think maybe I look at myself and I tell my wife,

1636
01:57:23.800 --> 01:57:32.160
I say, "I think we're responsible for a lot of this, because we made it too easy for them."

1637
01:57:32.160 --> 01:57:40.520
And we always use the alibi: I want them to do better than what we did, or live better.

1638
01:57:40.520 --> 01:57:45.800
And you give them all that and they take it for granted.

1639
01:57:45.800 --> 01:57:51.960
They want to get it quick, and they want to get it a lot.

1640
01:57:51.960 --> 01:57:55.480
They don't know what it means to sacrifice.

1641
01:57:55.480 --> 01:57:58.120
I don't want to see that.

1642
01:57:58.120 --> 01:58:06.040
I see it with most all of them now, my own kids, but they don't want to sacrifice.

1643
01:58:06.040 --> 01:58:07.800
So, I don't know.

1644
01:58:07.800 --> 01:58:12.200
<v DRAGOON>Thank you so much for your time today, Coach Williams.

1645
01:58:12.200 --> 01:58:15.280
<v WILLIAMS>I know there's something we probably missed.

1646
01:58:15.280 --> 01:58:17.920
We went two hours, didn't we?

1647
01:58:17.920 --> 01:58:19.240
<v DRAGOON>[Laughs] We did.

1648
01:58:19.240 --> 01:58:21.000
<v WILLIAMS>Probably went over.

1649
01:58:21.000 --> 01:58:21.762
<v DRAGOON>We did.

1650
01:58:21.762 --> 01:58:25.571
Is there anything else you'd like to add for today?

1651
01:58:25.571 --> 01:58:27.476
<v WILLIAMS>Nothing I can think of.

1652
01:58:27.476 --> 01:58:34.714
I know there's something we missed, but I think we got the majority of what we want, I guess.

1653
01:58:34.714 --> 01:58:35.095
<v DRAGOON>Alright.

1654
01:58:35.095 --> 01:58:36.619
Thank you so much.

1655
01:58:36.619 --> 01:58:37.762
<v WILLIAMS>We covered school.

1656
01:58:37.762 --> 01:58:38.905
We covered integration.

1657
01:58:38.905 --> 01:58:39.667
We covered . . .

1658
01:58:39.667 --> 01:58:41.190
<v DRAGOON>I could ask you . . .

1659
01:58:41.190 --> 01:58:41.952
<v WILLIAMS>. . . the community.

1660
01:58:41.952 --> 01:58:43.857
<v DRAGOON>. . . a million more questions [Laughs].

1661
01:58:43.857 --> 01:58:46.524
I have them all in my head.

1662
01:58:46.524 --> 01:58:51.095
Maybe we can come back another day if you're up for it.

1663
01:58:51.095 --> 01:58:54.524
<v WILLIAMS>Well, give me an idea what you're coming back.

1664
01:58:54.524 --> 01:58:59.857
Oh you don't have an idea now so I can be prepared for it.

1665
01:58:59.857 --> 01:59:00.619
<v DRAGOON>[Laughs] Alright.

1666
01:59:00.619 --> 01:59:02.143
Well, let's cut this.

1667
01:59:02.143 --> 01:59:04.429
<v WILLIAMS>You might think of something else.

1668
01:59:04.429 --> 01:59:06.714
If you do, call me back.

1669
01:59:06.714 --> 01:59:07.476
<v DRAGOON>Will do.

1670
01:59:07.476 --> 01:59:09.000
That sounds good.

1671
01:59:09.000 --> 01:59:09.200
<c.p>[End Tape 4551.

1672
01:59:09.000 --> 01:59:09.200
End Session I.]
